    /**
     * Generate descriptions for ALL services (old + new)
     */
    private function generateDescription(string $name): string
    {
        // ---------- FERTILITY DESCRIPTIONS ----------
        $fertilityDescriptions = [
            'In Vitro Fertilization (IVF)' => 'IVF is a fertility treatment where eggs are retrieved from the ovaries, fertilised with sperm in a laboratory, and the resulting embryos are transferred to the uterus. It is a widely used assisted reproductive technology for couples facing infertility due to blocked fallopian tubes, male factor infertility, ovulation disorders, or unexplained causes. The process involves ovarian stimulation, egg retrieval, fertilisation, embryo culture, and embryo transfer. IVF does not guarantee pregnancy, and success depends on individual factors such as age, egg quality, and overall health. This treatment is suitable for couples who have not conceived after trying for a year or more, or those with specific medical conditions affecting fertility. Patients are carefully monitored throughout the cycle with ultrasound scans and blood tests. Counselling and support are available to help you navigate the emotional aspects of treatment. Your fertility specialist will discuss your medical history and develop a personalised treatment plan tailored to your needs.',

            'Intrauterine Insemination (IUI)' => 'IUI is a simpler fertility treatment where prepared sperm is placed directly into the uterus around the time of ovulation. This procedure helps sperm reach the egg more easily and is often the first treatment option for couples with unexplained infertility, mild male factor infertility, or cervical mucus issues. The process involves monitoring your cycle to identify ovulation, collecting and washing a sperm sample, and inserting the prepared sperm into the uterus using a thin catheter. IUI is less invasive than IVF and is often combined with ovulation-inducing medication. It is suitable for women with open fallopian tubes and men with adequate sperm quality. The procedure is quick, usually painless, and does not require sedation. IUI may be repeated for several cycles. Success rates vary depending on age and underlying fertility factors. Your doctor will advise whether IUI is appropriate for your situation.',

            'Intracytoplasmic Sperm Injection (ICSI)' => 'ICSI is an advanced IVF technique where a single sperm is injected directly into an egg to achieve fertilisation. This procedure is particularly beneficial for couples with severe male factor infertility, including low sperm count, poor sperm motility, or abnormal sperm shape. ICSI is also used when previous IVF cycles have resulted in poor fertilisation. The process is similar to IVF, but the sperm selection and injection are performed under a microscope by an embryologist. ICSI increases the chances of fertilisation, but it does not guarantee pregnancy. This technique is often combined with IVF and is suitable for men who have undergone surgical sperm retrieval or those with obstructive azoospermia. Success rates depend on egg quality, embryo development, and the woman\'s age. Your fertility specialist will explain the process and help you understand if ICSI is the right option for you.',

            'Fertility Assessment & Consultation' => 'A comprehensive fertility assessment is the first step in understanding your reproductive health. This consultation involves a detailed review of your medical history, lifestyle factors, and any previous fertility investigations. Your fertility specialist will discuss your concerns, explain the available diagnostic tests, and outline potential treatment options. The consultation is a confidential and supportive space where you can ask questions and explore your fertility journey. For women, this may include a pelvic ultrasound and hormone blood tests. For men, a semen analysis is typically recommended. The assessment helps identify any underlying causes of infertility and guides the development of a personalised treatment plan. Early evaluation is recommended for couples who have been trying to conceive for over a year, or sooner if there are known risk factors. The goal is to provide clarity, reduce anxiety, and empower you with the information needed to make informed decisions about your fertility care.',

            'Female Infertility Diagnosis' => 'Female infertility diagnosis involves a series of tests to evaluate the reproductive system and identify factors that may be affecting fertility. Common causes include ovulation disorders, blocked fallopian tubes, endometriosis, fibroids, and hormonal imbalances. Diagnostic procedures may include pelvic ultrasound, hormone blood tests, ovarian reserve testing, hysterosalpingography (HSG) to check tubal patency, and hysteroscopy to examine the uterine cavity. These tests help your fertility specialist understand the specific challenges you are facing and develop a targeted treatment plan. The diagnosis process is thorough and tailored to your individual needs. It is important to remember that infertility is a medical condition, not a personal failure. Many causes of female infertility can be treated effectively. Your doctor will explain each test, what it involves, and how the results will inform your treatment options.',

            'Male Infertility Diagnosis' => 'Male infertility diagnosis evaluates the factors that may be affecting sperm production, function, or delivery. The primary test is semen analysis, which assesses sperm count, motility, morphology, and the presence of infections. Additional tests may include hormone blood tests, genetic screening, ultrasound imaging of the reproductive organs, and, in some cases, testicular biopsy. Male factor infertility contributes to about one-third of all infertility cases, and it is important that both partners undergo evaluation. Common causes include low sperm production, poor sperm quality, blockages, or hormonal imbalances. The diagnostic process is designed to identify the specific issue and determine the most appropriate treatment, which may range from lifestyle changes and medication to surgical sperm retrieval or IVF with ICSI. A thorough evaluation provides a clear pathway forward and helps set realistic expectations for treatment.',

            'Hormonal Profiling' => 'Hormonal profiling involves a series of blood tests that measure the levels of key reproductive hormones. For women, this typically includes FSH, LH, estradiol, progesterone, AMH, and thyroid hormones. For men, testosterone, FSH, and LH are commonly assessed. These tests help evaluate ovarian reserve, ovulation function, and the health of the pituitary and thyroid glands. Hormonal imbalances can affect fertility by disrupting ovulation or sperm production. The results provide essential information for diagnosis and treatment planning. Hormonal profiling is a simple blood test, usually performed at specific times in the menstrual cycle. Your fertility specialist will interpret the results in the context of your overall health and medical history. This information guides decisions about ovulation induction, IVF protocols, and other fertility treatments.',

            'Ovarian Reserve Assessment' => 'Ovarian reserve assessment is a key fertility test that estimates the number and quality of a woman\'s remaining eggs. It typically involves a blood test to measure Anti-Müllerian Hormone (AMH) levels and an ultrasound to count antral follicles (AFC). These tests provide valuable information about a woman\'s reproductive potential and help predict how she may respond to ovarian stimulation during IVF. Ovarian reserve naturally declines with age, but it can also be affected by medical conditions, surgeries, or treatments like chemotherapy. The assessment helps your fertility specialist tailor treatment protocols and set realistic expectations. It is not a measure of egg quality or a guarantee of pregnancy, but it is a useful tool for personalised care. The tests are simple, non-invasive, and can be performed on any day of the menstrual cycle.',

            'Semen Analysis' => 'Semen analysis is the cornerstone of male fertility evaluation. It measures the volume, sperm count, motility (movement), and morphology (shape) of sperm in a semen sample. This test helps identify issues such as low sperm count, poor motility, or abnormal sperm shape, which can affect a couple\'s ability to conceive. The sample is collected by masturbation after 2-5 days of abstinence and analysed in a laboratory. Results are usually available within a few days. Semen analysis is a simple, non-invasive test that provides crucial information for fertility diagnosis. If abnormalities are detected, further tests may be recommended to determine the underlying cause. Your fertility specialist will discuss the results and explain how they impact your treatment options.',

            'Ovulation Induction & Monitoring' => 'Ovulation induction involves using medication to stimulate the ovaries to produce one or more eggs. This is often the first line of treatment for women with irregular or absent ovulation, such as those with PCOS. The process is monitored with ultrasound scans and blood tests to track follicle development and determine the optimal timing for intercourse or insemination. Ovulation induction can be used alone or in combination with IUI or timed intercourse. It is a less invasive and more affordable option compared to IVF. The treatment is carefully controlled to minimise the risk of multiple pregnancies and ovarian hyperstimulation. Your fertility specialist will guide you through each step, adjusting medication doses as needed based on your response.',

            'Follicular Tracking for Timed Intercourse' => 'Follicular tracking is a series of ultrasound scans performed during your menstrual cycle to monitor the growth and development of ovarian follicles. This helps identify the most fertile window for timed intercourse or insemination. The scans are usually performed every 2-3 days starting around day 8-10 of your cycle. The procedure is painless and involves a transvaginal ultrasound. Follicular tracking is often used for couples with irregular cycles or those who want to maximise their chances of conception without more invasive treatments. It can also be combined with ovulation induction medication. The results help you and your partner time intercourse for the best chance of pregnancy. This is a supportive, non‑invasive approach that empowers couples with knowledge about their cycle.',

            'Egg Cryopreservation (Egg Freezing)' => 'Egg freezing, or oocyte cryopreservation, is a method of preserving a woman\'s fertility by extracting, freezing, and storing her eggs for future use. This is an option for women who wish to delay childbearing due to career, personal circumstances, or medical reasons such as cancer treatment that may affect fertility. The process involves ovarian stimulation, egg retrieval, and vitrification (rapid freezing) to preserve egg quality. Stored eggs can be thawed, fertilised with sperm, and transferred as embryos in the future. Egg freezing does not guarantee a future pregnancy, and success depends on the woman\'s age at the time of freezing and the quality of the eggs. It is a proactive choice that offers women greater reproductive flexibility. Your fertility specialist will discuss the process, success rates, and considerations before you decide.',

            'Sperm Cryopreservation (Sperm Freezing)' => 'Sperm freezing is a well-established method of preserving male fertility. A semen sample is collected, processed, and frozen using specialised techniques that protect sperm quality. Frozen sperm can be stored for many years and used for fertility treatments such as IUI, IVF, or ICSI. This is beneficial for men facing medical treatments that may impact fertility, those undergoing vasectomy, or men with occupational or lifestyle factors that may reduce sperm quality over time. Sperm freezing is also used for sperm donation programmes. The process is straightforward and does not require any special preparation beyond providing a semen sample. Stored sperm can be thawed and used when you are ready to start a family. It is a reliable and widely available fertility preservation option.',

            'Embryo Cryopreservation (Embryo Freezing)' => 'Embryo freezing is the process of preserving embryos (fertilised eggs) for future use. After IVF or ICSI, any embryos that are not transferred immediately can be frozen and stored. This allows couples to have additional attempts at pregnancy without undergoing a full IVF cycle. Embryos are frozen using vitrification, a rapid freezing technique that minimises damage. Stored embryos can be thawed and transferred in a subsequent cycle. Embryo freezing is also used in egg donation and surrogacy arrangements. It offers flexibility and can improve the overall efficiency of fertility treatment. Success rates for frozen embryo transfers are comparable to fresh transfers. Your fertility specialist will explain the process, storage options, and legal considerations.',

            'Egg Donation Programme' => 'Egg donation enables women who cannot use their own eggs to achieve pregnancy. This may be due to premature ovarian failure, genetic disorders, or advanced age. Our egg donation programme involves carefully screened donors who undergo medical and psychological evaluation. Donor eggs are fertilised with sperm from the intended father or a sperm donor, and the resulting embryos are transferred to the recipient\'s uterus. Egg donation offers hope to many individuals and couples who would otherwise be unable to conceive. The programme is built on ethical practices and includes counselling for both donors and recipients. Legal agreements are handled to protect all parties. Success rates with donor eggs are generally high. Your fertility specialist will guide you through the process and help you understand the medical, emotional, and legal aspects.',

            'Sperm Donation Programme' => 'Sperm donation is a treatment option for couples where the male partner has severe infertility, for single women, or for female couples. Our sperm donation programme uses carefully screened donors who undergo medical, genetic, and psychological assessments. Donor sperm can be used for IUI, IVF, or ICSI. The programme prioritises safety, transparency, and ethical practice. Recipients receive detailed information about the donor, including medical history and physical characteristics, to help them make an informed choice. Counselling is available for both donors and recipients. Sperm donation is a straightforward and effective way to build a family. Your fertility specialist will explain the process and help you select a donor that meets your needs.',

            'Surrogacy Arrangements' => 'Surrogacy is a reproductive arrangement where a woman carries a pregnancy for intended parents. This may be an option for couples where the woman cannot carry a pregnancy due to medical reasons, or for single men and same-sex male couples. Our surrogacy programme involves careful screening of surrogates, legal agreements, and medical oversight. We provide support for all parties throughout the journey, including counselling and coordination of medical care. Surrogacy is a complex process that requires careful planning and legal guidance. Our team works with experienced legal professionals to ensure the process is smooth and compliant with relevant regulations. The programme is designed to be compassionate, transparent, and supportive for everyone involved.',

            'Embryo Donation' => 'Embryo donation allows couples who have completed their family to donate their remaining frozen embryos to others who are trying to conceive. This is a generous option that can provide hope to individuals and couples facing infertility. Recipients undergo a thorough evaluation to ensure they are suitable candidates. The donated embryos are transferred to the recipient\'s uterus in a frozen embryo transfer cycle. Embryo donation involves legal agreements and counselling for both donors and recipients. It is a meaningful way to help others achieve their dream of parenthood. Your fertility specialist will explain the process and support you through every step.',

            'PESA (Percutaneous Epididymal Sperm Aspiration)' => 'PESA is a minimally invasive procedure used to retrieve sperm from the epididymis for men with obstructive azoospermia (a blockage preventing sperm from being ejaculated). A fine needle is inserted through the skin into the epididymis to aspirate sperm. The procedure is performed under local anaesthesia and takes about 15-20 minutes. Retrieved sperm are used immediately for ICSI. PESA is less invasive than open surgical procedures and has a shorter recovery time. It is suitable for men with normal sperm production but a blockage. The procedure is performed by an experienced urologist or fertility specialist. You may experience mild discomfort for a few days. Your doctor will provide detailed instructions for recovery.',

            'TESA (Testicular Sperm Aspiration)' => 'TESA is a procedure that retrieves sperm directly from the testicles using a fine needle. It is used for men with obstructive or non-obstructive azoospermia. The procedure is performed under local or general anaesthesia, depending on the case. A small needle is inserted into the testicle to aspirate tissue and sperm. The retrieved sperm are used for ICSI. TESA is a less invasive option compared to TESE and has a shorter recovery period. It is suitable for men who have sperm production but a blockage or reduced sperm output. The procedure takes about 15-20 minutes. Your fertility specialist will discuss the risks and benefits. Recovery is usually quick, with mild discomfort manageable with simple pain relief.',

            'TESE (Testicular Sperm Extraction)' => 'TESE is a surgical procedure that involves removing a small sample of testicular tissue to extract sperm. It is used for men with non-obstructive azoospermia, where sperm production is severely reduced. The procedure is performed under general anaesthesia. A small incision is made in the testicle, and a tissue sample is taken and examined for sperm. If sperm are found, they are used for ICSI. TESE is more invasive than TESA or PESA but may be the only option for some men. Recovery takes a few days, and you may need to avoid strenuous activity. The success of sperm retrieval depends on the underlying cause. Your fertility specialist will explain the procedure and help you understand the chances of finding sperm.',

            'MESA (Microsurgical Epididymal Sperm Extraction)' => 'MESA is a microsurgical procedure performed under an operating microscope to retrieve sperm from the epididymis. It is used for men with obstructive azoospermia. The procedure is performed under general anaesthesia. A small incision is made in the scrotum, and the epididymis is examined under a microscope. Sperm are aspirated from the epididymal tubules. MESA allows for the retrieval of a larger number of sperm compared to PESA. It is often used when PESA is not successful or when a larger sperm sample is needed. Recovery takes about a week. The procedure is performed by a skilled urologist. Your fertility specialist will discuss the risks, benefits, and recovery process in detail.',

            'Vasectomy Reversal' => 'Vasectomy reversal is a surgical procedure that reconnects the vas deferens, the tubes that carry sperm from the testicles. This allows sperm to be present in the ejaculate again, potentially restoring fertility. The procedure is performed under general anaesthesia and involves microsurgical techniques to reconnect the tubes. Success depends on factors such as the time since the vasectomy, the quality of the sperm, and the surgical technique. Vasectomy reversal is an option for men who wish to have children after a vasectomy. Recovery takes a few weeks, and you should avoid heavy lifting. Your urologist will discuss the chances of success and the risks involved. The procedure does not guarantee pregnancy, but it can restore fertility in many cases.',

            'Varicocelectomy' => 'Varicocelectomy is a surgical procedure to treat varicocele, a condition where veins in the scrotum become enlarged. Varicocele can affect sperm production and quality, leading to male infertility. The surgery involves tying off the affected veins to redirect blood flow. It is performed under general or local anaesthesia. Varicocelectomy can improve sperm count and motility in some men, increasing the chances of natural conception or improving the success of IVF/ICSI. Recovery takes about 2-4 weeks. Your urologist will discuss the risks and benefits. This procedure is suitable for men with a clinically significant varicocele and abnormal semen parameters.',

            'TURED (Transurethral Resection of Ejaculatory Ducts)' => 'TURED is a procedure to treat ejaculatory duct obstruction, a condition that can cause low semen volume or azoospermia. It involves inserting a resectoscope through the urethra to remove the blockage. The procedure is performed under general or spinal anaesthesia. TURED can restore the flow of sperm and improve semen parameters. It is suitable for men with confirmed ejaculatory duct obstruction. Recovery takes a few days, and you may experience blood in the urine or semen temporarily. Your urologist will explain the procedure and the expected outcomes.',

            'Hysteroscopy' => 'Hysteroscopy is a procedure that allows your doctor to look inside your uterus using a thin, lighted telescope called a hysteroscope. It is used to diagnose and treat conditions such as fibroids, polyps, adhesions, and abnormal bleeding. The procedure is performed under local or general anaesthesia. A hysteroscope is inserted through the vagina and cervix into the uterus. If abnormalities are found, they can often be treated during the same procedure. Hysteroscopy is a valuable tool for investigating infertility, recurrent miscarriage, or abnormal uterine bleeding. Recovery is usually quick, with mild cramping or discharge for a few days. Your gynaecologist will discuss the findings and any further treatment that may be needed.',

            'Dilation & Curettage (D&C)' => 'D&C is a procedure to remove tissue from the inside of the uterus. It is used to diagnose or treat conditions such as abnormal bleeding, miscarriage, or endometrial polyps. The procedure involves dilating the cervix and using a curette (a surgical instrument) to scrape the uterine lining. D&C is performed under general or local anaesthesia. It is a common and safe procedure that can provide relief from symptoms and help diagnose underlying conditions. Recovery takes a few days, and you may experience mild cramping and bleeding. Your gynaecologist will explain the reasons for the procedure and what to expect during recovery.',

            'LEEP (Loop Electrosurgical Excision Procedure)' => 'LEEP is a procedure used to remove abnormal tissue from the cervix. It is often performed after an abnormal Pap smear to treat cervical dysplasia (pre-cancerous changes). A thin wire loop with an electrical current is used to remove the abnormal tissue. LEEP is performed under local anaesthesia and takes about 15-20 minutes. It is a safe and effective procedure that can prevent the progression of cervical cancer. Recovery takes a few weeks, and you should avoid sexual intercourse and tampons during this time. Your gynaecologist will discuss the results and any follow-up care that is needed.',

            'Laparoscopy' => 'Laparoscopy is a minimally invasive surgical procedure that uses a small camera to examine the pelvic organs. It is used to diagnose and treat conditions such as endometriosis, fibroids, ovarian cysts, and pelvic adhesions. Small incisions are made in the abdomen, and a laparoscope is inserted to visualise the organs. If abnormalities are found, they can often be treated during the same procedure. Laparoscopy is performed under general anaesthesia. Recovery is usually quicker than open surgery, with less pain and scarring. It is a valuable tool for investigating infertility and pelvic pain. Your gynaecologist will explain the procedure, risks, and recovery.',

            'Hysterectomy' => 'Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the uterus. It is performed for conditions such as fibroids, endometriosis, pelvic organ prolapse, abnormal bleeding, or cancer. The surgery can be performed through the vagina (vaginal hysterectomy), abdomen (abdominal hysterectomy), or laparoscopically. Recovery time depends on the type of surgery. Hysterectomy is a major procedure that results in the permanent inability to conceive. It is only recommended when other treatments have failed or when there is a medical necessity. Your gynaecologist will discuss all options and help you make an informed decision.',

            'Colposcopy' => 'Colposcopy is a procedure that uses a special microscope (colposcope) to examine the cervix, vagina, and vulva for abnormal cells. It is usually performed after an abnormal Pap smear. A colposcope is placed at the opening of the vagina, and the cervix is examined with a magnified view. If abnormal areas are seen, a biopsy may be taken for further testing. Colposcopy is a quick and relatively painless procedure that helps detect cervical cancer early. Recovery is usually minimal, with some mild spotting. Your gynaecologist will explain the results and any follow-up care that may be needed.',

            'Ovarian Cystectomy' => 'Ovarian cystectomy is a surgical procedure to remove a cyst from the ovary while preserving healthy ovarian tissue. It is performed for cysts that are large, painful, or causing symptoms. The surgery can be performed laparoscopically or through an open incision. Recovery time depends on the type of surgery. Ovarian cystectomy is a fertility-preserving procedure that allows women to maintain ovarian function. Your gynaecologist will discuss the reasons for the surgery and what to expect during recovery.',

            'Endometriosis Treatment' => 'Endometriosis occurs when tissue similar to the lining of the uterus grows outside the uterus, causing pain and infertility. Treatment options include medication to manage pain and hormone therapy to suppress the growth of endometrial tissue. Surgical options, such as laparoscopy, can remove endometrial implants. The choice of treatment depends on the severity of symptoms and your fertility goals. Your gynaecologist will discuss the best approach for your individual situation. Early diagnosis and treatment can improve symptoms and fertility outcomes.',

            'Fibroid Treatment' => 'Fibroids are non-cancerous growths in the uterus that can cause heavy bleeding, pain, and infertility. Treatment options include medication to manage symptoms, uterine artery embolisation (UAE) to shrink fibroids, or surgical removal (myomectomy) to preserve fertility. In severe cases, hysterectomy may be recommended if childbearing is not a concern. The choice of treatment depends on the size, location, and symptoms of the fibroids, as well as your fertility goals. Your gynaecologist will discuss all options and help you make an informed decision.',

            'Pelvic Organ Prolapse Treatment' => 'Pelvic organ prolapse occurs when pelvic organs (bladder, uterus, or rectum) drop from their normal position due to weakened pelvic floor muscles. Treatment options include pelvic floor exercises, vaginal pessaries, or surgery. Surgical options include vaginal or abdominal procedures to repair the prolapse. The choice of treatment depends on the severity of symptoms and your overall health. Your gynaecologist will discuss the best approach for your individual situation. Early treatment can improve symptoms and quality of life.',

            'Abnormal Bleeding Treatment' => 'Abnormal bleeding, such as heavy or irregular periods, can be caused by hormonal imbalances, fibroids, polyps, or other conditions. Treatment options include medication to regulate hormones, non-surgical procedures (e.g., endometrial ablation), or surgery (e.g., hysterectomy) in severe cases. The choice of treatment depends on the cause and severity of the bleeding, as well as your fertility goals. Your gynaecologist will discuss the best approach for your individual situation. Proper diagnosis and treatment can improve your quality of life.',

            'Permanent Birth Control' => 'Permanent birth control, also known as sterilisation, is a procedure that permanently prevents pregnancy. For women, this involves tubal ligation (blocking the fallopian tubes) or hysterectomy. For men, a vasectomy is the standard procedure. These procedures are intended for individuals who have completed their family or who do not wish to have children. They are irreversible, so careful consideration and counselling are essential. Your doctor will discuss the procedure, risks, and alternatives to help you make an informed decision.',

            'Fertility Counselling' => 'Fertility counselling provides emotional support and guidance for individuals and couples navigating the challenges of infertility. It helps you understand the emotional impact of fertility treatment, manage stress, and develop coping strategies. Counselling is a confidential space where you can discuss your feelings, concerns, and treatment decisions. It is an integral part of fertility care, helping you maintain mental well-being throughout your journey. Our counsellors are experienced in fertility-related issues and provide a compassionate, non-judgmental environment. They can also help with communication between partners and decision-making about treatment options.',

            'Psychological Support for Fertility Patients' => 'The journey to parenthood can be emotionally demanding, and psychological support is essential for managing the stress, anxiety, and uncertainty that often accompany fertility treatment. Our psychological support services are designed to help you navigate the emotional weight of the trying-to-conceive journey. We offer individual and couples counselling, stress management techniques, and support groups. Our team is experienced in addressing the unique psychological challenges of infertility, including the impact on relationships, self-esteem, and mental health. We are here to support you every step of the way, ensuring you feel heard, understood, and empowered.',

            'Patient Education & Cycle Tracking' => 'Understanding your fertility treatment is an important part of the journey. We provide comprehensive patient education on fertility, treatment options, and cycle tracking. This includes guidance on monitoring your menstrual cycle, identifying ovulation, and understanding the timing of treatments. Our team will explain the science behind fertility in simple, accessible language, so you feel confident and informed. We also provide resources and tools to help you track your progress and stay organised throughout your treatment.',

            'Medication & Self-Administration Guidance' => 'Fertility treatment often involves medications that require careful administration. Our team provides detailed guidance on medication types, dosages, and administration techniques. We will show you how to prepare and inject medications, manage side effects, and store medications properly. We are here to answer any questions and provide support throughout your treatment. Our goal is to ensure you feel confident and capable of managing your medication regimen, reducing stress and improving treatment outcomes.',

            'Preimplantation Genetic Testing (PGT)' => 'Preimplantation Genetic Testing (PGT) is a technique used to screen embryos for genetic abnormalities before transfer. It can identify chromosomal abnormalities (PGT-A) or specific genetic disorders (PGT-M). PGT is performed on embryos created through IVF and involves removing a few cells for genetic analysis. Only embryos that are genetically normal are selected for transfer. This can improve the chances of a successful pregnancy and reduce the risk of miscarriage or genetic disorders. PGT is suitable for couples with a known genetic condition, advanced maternal age, or recurrent miscarriage. Your fertility specialist will discuss whether PGT is appropriate for your situation.',

            'Assisted Embryo Hatching with Laser' => 'Assisted embryo hatching is a technique used to help embryos implant in the uterus. A small opening is made in the outer shell (zona pellucida) of the embryo using a laser, making it easier for the embryo to hatch and attach to the uterine lining. This procedure may improve implantation rates, particularly for women with advanced age or previous failed IVF cycles. It is performed in the laboratory by an embryologist. Assisted hatching is a safe and widely used technique. Your fertility specialist will discuss whether it is suitable for you.',

            'Non-Invasive Prenatal Testing (NIPT)' => 'Non-Invasive Prenatal Testing (NIPT) is a blood test that screens for chromosomal abnormalities in a developing baby. It is performed from 10 weeks of pregnancy and can detect conditions such as Down syndrome, Edwards syndrome, and Patau syndrome. NIPT is highly accurate and carries no risk to the pregnancy. It is a valuable tool for prenatal screening and provides early information about the baby\'s health. A positive result does not confirm a diagnosis, and further testing such as amniocentesis may be recommended. Your doctor will discuss the results and any follow-up steps.',

            'Early Pregnancy Scan' => 'An early pregnancy scan is an ultrasound performed in the first trimester to confirm pregnancy, check the baby\'s heartbeat, and estimate the due date. It can also detect multiple pregnancies and identify early complications such as ectopic pregnancy. The scan is usually performed transvaginally and is painless. It provides reassurance and important information for your pregnancy care. Your obstetrician will discuss the findings and plan your ongoing care.',

            'Reproductive Monitoring' => 'Reproductive monitoring involves regular ultrasound scans and blood tests to track your reproductive cycle and treatment progress. It is used in fertility treatments to monitor follicle growth, ovulation, and uterine lining development. The monitoring helps your fertility specialist adjust treatment protocols and timing for optimal results. It is a key part of IVF, IUI, and ovulation induction cycles. The tests are simple and non-invasive, providing essential information to guide your care.',
        ];

        // ---------- ORIGINAL SERVICE DESCRIPTIONS ----------
        $originalDescriptions = [
            'RVS' => 'The RVS (or Routine Venous Screen) is a comprehensive blood test used to evaluate multiple health parameters in a single draw. It typically includes a full blood count, basic metabolic panel, and lipid profile, offering a broad overview of a patient\'s current health status. This test is often ordered as part of an annual physical examination or pre-employment screening. The procedure involves a simple venipuncture, usually from the arm, and requires minimal preparation—often just a few hours of fasting. Results are available within 24 to 48 hours and provide valuable insights into red and white blood cell counts, electrolyte balance, kidney and liver function, and cholesterol levels. The RVS is a cost-effective way to detect early signs of anemia, infection, diabetes, and cardiovascular risk. It also serves as a baseline for monitoring chronic conditions. Because it covers so many aspects of health, the RVS is one of the most commonly requested laboratory tests in general medicine. Patients should expect a comfortable experience, with a trained phlebotomist ensuring safety and minimal discomfort. The test is suitable for all age groups and can be repeated periodically to track changes over time. Always interpret results in consultation with a qualified healthcare provider.',

            'HEPATITIS A,B,C' => 'The Hepatitis A, B, and C panel is a combined serological test that detects current or past infections with the three major hepatitis viruses. Hepatitis A is usually self-limiting and transmitted via the fecal-oral route, while Hepatitis B and C are bloodborne viruses that can cause chronic liver disease, cirrhosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma. This panel measures specific antibodies and antigens: anti-HAV IgM for acute Hepatitis A, HBsAg and anti-HBc for Hepatitis B, and anti-HCV for Hepatitis C. The test is essential for diagnosing the cause of elevated liver enzymes, jaundice, or unexplained fatigue. It is also routinely performed before surgery, during pregnancy, and for occupational health screening. The procedure involves drawing a venous blood sample; no special preparation is required. Results are typically available within 1–2 days. A positive result for Hepatitis B or C requires further quantitative testing (viral load) and specialist consultation. Early detection is crucial for initiating antiviral therapy, reducing transmission risk, and preventing long-term complications. The test is safe and carries minimal risk. Patients should discuss their vaccination status and exposure history with their doctor when interpreting results.',

            'FBS/RBS' => 'The Fasting Blood Sugar (FBS) and Random Blood Sugar (RBS) tests measure the concentration of glucose in the blood at a given time. FBS is performed after an overnight fast of 8–12 hours and is the primary screening tool for diabetes mellitus and prediabetes. RBS can be taken at any time regardless of food intake and is useful for monitoring glucose control in known diabetics or in emergency settings. These tests are simple, rapid, and widely available. For FBS, a fasting blood sample is drawn from a vein; for RBS, a fingerstick or venous sample is used. Normal fasting glucose is typically below 5.6 mmol/L (100 mg/dL), while values above 7.0 mmol/L (126 mg/dL) indicate diabetes. The RBS normal range is usually under 11.1 mmol/L (200 mg/dL) in asymptomatic individuals. Patients are advised to avoid excessive carbohydrate intake before fasting. The test is painless, with results often available within minutes. Regular monitoring is essential for managing diabetes, adjusting medication, and preventing complications such as neuropathy, retinopathy, and kidney disease. All results should be interpreted by a physician in the context of the patient\'s clinical history.',

            'P.C.V' => 'The Packed Cell Volume (PCV) test, also known as haematocrit, measures the proportion of red blood cells in a blood sample. It is a simple, quick, and widely used test to evaluate anaemia, polycythaemia, and hydration status. A small blood sample is collected into a heparinized capillary tube, centrifuged to separate the cells from the plasma, and the percentage of red cells is read using a microhaematocrit reader. Normal values vary by age and sex: adult males typically 40–52%, females 36–48%. Low PCV indicates anaemia due to causes such as iron deficiency, blood loss, or bone marrow disorders, while high PCV may be seen in dehydration, smoking, or polycythaemia vera. The test requires minimal preparation, but patients should inform their doctor about any medications that may affect blood cell counts. Results are available within an hour. The PCV is often done alongside haemoglobin measurement and red cell indices. It is a cost-effective screening tool that helps guide further diagnostic testing. Patients with abnormal results should undergo a complete blood count and additional investigations to determine the underlying cause. The procedure is safe, with only a slight discomfort from the finger prick or venipuncture.',

            'FULL BLOOD COUNT' => 'The Full Blood Count (FBC) is a comprehensive hematological test that evaluates the three main types of cells in the blood: red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets. It provides crucial information about a patient\'s overall health and can detect a wide range of disorders, including anemia, infection, inflammation, and clotting abnormalities. The test is typically performed on a venous blood sample and involves automated cell counting and differential analysis. Patients can expect a simple blood draw, with results available within a few hours. The FBC is one of the most commonly ordered laboratory tests and is essential for both routine check-ups and diagnostic investigations. It helps clinicians monitor chronic conditions, assess the efficacy of treatments, and guide further diagnostic testing. In addition to cell counts, the FBC includes parameters such as hemoglobin, hematocrit, and mean corpuscular volume, each providing valuable insights into red cell morphology and function. White cell differential counts assist in identifying the type of infection or immune response. Platelet counts are critical for assessing bleeding risk. The test is safe, with minimal discomfort, and is performed in a certified medical laboratory by trained professionals. Regular monitoring with FBC can aid in early detection of hematological malignancies and other systemic diseases. Results must be interpreted in the clinical context.',

            'M.P & WIDAL' => 'The Malaria Parasite (MP) test and Widal test are two separate but commonly paired investigations for febrile illnesses. The MP test detects the presence of Plasmodium species in the blood, either through thick and thin blood smears or rapid diagnostic tests (RDTs) that identify specific antigens. It is the gold standard for diagnosing malaria, a life-threatening parasitic disease transmitted by infected mosquitoes. The Widal test is a serological assay that detects antibodies against Salmonella typhi and paratyphi, the bacteria responsible for typhoid fever. It is often used in endemic areas to support the diagnosis of enteric fever. Both tests require a venous blood sample, though RDTs may use a finger prick. No fasting is required. Results are usually available within a few hours. The MP test is highly sensitive when performed by experienced microscopists, while the Widal test has limitations due to cross-reactivity and varying background titers in endemic regions. Therefore, clinical correlation is essential. Patients with positive results should receive appropriate antimicrobial or antimalarial therapy and supportive care. These tests are vital for prompt diagnosis and management of common infections in tropical settings.',

            'URINALYSIS' => 'Urinalysis is a comprehensive examination of urine that provides valuable information about kidney function, urinary tract infections, and systemic diseases such as diabetes and liver disorders. The test is divided into three parts: physical examination (color, clarity, odor), chemical analysis (using dipstick for pH, protein, glucose, ketones, bilirubin, urobilinogen, nitrite, and leukocyte esterase), and microscopic examination (looking for red blood cells, white blood cells, casts, crystals, and bacteria). The procedure is non-invasive; patients simply provide a mid-stream clean-catch urine sample in a sterile container. No special preparation is required, though patients may be asked to avoid excessive fluids beforehand. Results are available within 1–2 hours. Abnormal findings can indicate infections, dehydration, kidney stones, glomerulonephritis, or metabolic conditions. For example, the presence of nitrites and leukocyte esterase suggests a bacterial infection, while glucose and ketones may point to uncontrolled diabetes. Microscopic haematuria may require further imaging or cytology. Urinalysis is a cost-effective, rapid screening tool used in almost every clinical setting. Patients should follow proper collection instructions to avoid contamination and ensure accurate results.',

            'URINE M/C/S & URETHRAL SWAB M/C/S' => 'This combined test includes urine culture and sensitivity (C/S) and urethral swab culture and sensitivity. The urine C/S is used to diagnose urinary tract infections (UTIs) by identifying the causative organism and determining its antibiotic susceptibility. A clean-catch midstream urine sample is collected in a sterile container and incubated on appropriate media. The urethral swab is collected by inserting a sterile swab into the urethra to obtain a sample for culture, primarily to detect sexually transmitted infections such as Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis, as well as other urethral pathogens. Both procedures are essential for targeted antimicrobial therapy. For urine collection, patients should clean the external genitalia and collect the midstream portion. For the swab, there may be mild discomfort. Results typically take 48–72 hours for culture and sensitivity. These tests are crucial for managing persistent or recurrent UTIs, sexually transmitted diseases, and for guiding empirical antibiotic choice. Patients should avoid antibiotics before sample collection to ensure accurate results. All findings should be correlated with clinical symptoms and imaging if necessary.',

            'B.P CHECK' => 'Blood pressure (BP) measurement is a simple, non-invasive procedure that assesses the force of blood against the walls of arteries. It is a fundamental vital sign used to screen for hypertension (high blood pressure) and hypotension (low blood pressure). The test is performed using a sphygmomanometer and stethoscope or an automated oscillometric device. A cuff is placed around the upper arm, inflated to temporarily stop blood flow, and then slowly deflated while listening for Korotkoff sounds or reading the digital display. Systolic pressure (the top number) represents the pressure during heart contractions, while diastolic pressure (the bottom number) is the pressure between beats. Normal resting BP is approximately 120/80 mmHg. Elevated readings may indicate hypertension, which is a major risk factor for heart disease, stroke, and kidney failure. The test is quick, painless, and can be done in any clinical setting. Patients should sit quietly for 5 minutes before measurement, avoid caffeine and smoking, and use a properly sized cuff. Multiple readings over time are needed for diagnosis. Home monitoring is also beneficial. Early detection and lifestyle modifications can prevent complications.',

            'E.C.G' => 'An Electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG) is a non-invasive test that records the electrical activity of the heart over a period of time. It is used to detect cardiac arrhythmias, myocardial ischemia, infarction, conduction abnormalities, and hypertrophy. The test involves placing 10 electrodes on the patient\'s chest, arms, and legs, which pick up the tiny electrical signals generated by the heart\'s depolarization and repolarization. The resulting waveform is displayed on a screen or printed on paper. The procedure takes about 5–10 minutes and is entirely painless. No special preparation is required, though patients may be asked to remove jewelry and avoid lotions. The ECG provides immediate information about heart rate, rhythm, axis, and intervals. Abnormalities such as ST-segment elevation or depression, T-wave inversion, or pathological Q waves can indicate acute or prior myocardial infarction. The test is essential in emergency departments, pre-operative assessments, and cardiology clinics. While a normal ECG does not rule out all cardiac conditions, it is a powerful first-line tool. Further testing such as echocardiography or stress testing may follow if abnormalities are found.',

            'SYPHILLIS TEST' => 'The Syphilis test detects antibodies produced by the body in response to Treponema pallidum, the bacterium that causes syphilis. This sexually transmitted infection progresses through primary, secondary, latent, and tertiary stages if untreated. Screening typically involves non-treponemal tests such as RPR (Rapid Plasma Reagin) or VDRL (Venereal Disease Research Laboratory), which are sensitive but not specific, and treponemal-specific tests like FTA-ABS or TPHA for confirmation. The test is performed on a blood sample; no fasting is required. Results are available within 1–2 days. Positive screening tests must be confirmed with a treponemal test to differentiate from false positives caused by other conditions such as lupus, pregnancy, or Lyme disease. Early treatment with antibiotics (penicillin) can cure the infection and prevent serious complications like neurosyphilis or congenital syphilis in pregnant women. Routine screening is recommended for high-risk groups and during pregnancy. Patients with suspected exposure should undergo testing regardless of symptoms. Counseling and partner notification are integral parts of management. The test is safe, and prompt diagnosis is key to public health control.',

            'A-P SCAN' => 'The Abdomino-Pelvic (A-P) Scan is an ultrasound examination that combines imaging of both the abdomen and the pelvic region. It provides a comprehensive evaluation of the liver, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, kidneys, urinary bladder, uterus, ovaries, prostate, and surrounding structures. This scan is useful for assessing abdominal pain, distension, masses, hepatobiliary disease, renal stones, and pelvic pathology. The procedure is non-invasive and involves applying a water-based gel to the skin and moving a transducer over the abdomen and lower pelvis. Patients are usually required to fast for 6–8 hours before the scan to reduce bowel gas and ensure a full bladder for pelvic imaging. The test takes about 30–45 minutes and is painless. It can detect conditions such as gallstones, liver tumors, kidney cysts, uterine fibroids, and ovarian cysts. It is also used to guide biopsies or drainage procedures. The scan is safe for all patients, including pregnant women. Results are interpreted by a radiologist or sonologist and are available within a few hours. It is a valuable diagnostic tool with no radiation exposure.',

            'CHOLESTORL' => 'The Cholesterol test, often part of a lipid profile, measures the total cholesterol level in the blood. Cholesterol is a waxy substance necessary for cell membrane formation and hormone synthesis, but elevated levels are a major risk factor for atherosclerosis and cardiovascular disease. The test is performed on a blood sample after a fast of 9–12 hours to obtain accurate results. Total cholesterol includes LDL (low-density lipoprotein, the "bad" cholesterol), HDL (high-density lipoprotein, the "good" cholesterol), and triglycerides. Optimal total cholesterol is below 5.2 mmol/L (200 mg/dL). High levels may require lifestyle modifications (diet, exercise) and possibly statin therapy. The test is simple and widely available. Patients should avoid alcohol and fatty foods before the test. Results are typically available within 24 hours. It is recommended that adults have their cholesterol checked every 4–6 years, or more frequently if at risk. The test helps guide primary and secondary prevention of heart attacks and strokes. Interpretation should consider age, gender, and other risk factors. The test is safe and carries minimal risk.',

            'URIC ACID' => 'The Uric Acid test measures the level of uric acid in the blood, a waste product formed from the breakdown of purines (substances found in certain foods and body tissues). Elevated uric acid can lead to gout, a painful form of arthritis, and may also be associated with kidney stones and kidney disease. The test is performed on a venous blood sample; patients may be asked to fast for 4–8 hours and avoid alcohol and purine-rich foods (such as organ meats and shellfish) before testing. Normal ranges are typically 3.4–7.0 mg/dL for men and 2.4–6.0 mg/dL for women. High levels require further evaluation and may be managed with dietary changes and medications like allopurinol. The test is also used to monitor patients receiving chemotherapy, as rapid cell destruction can raise uric acid levels (tumor lysis syndrome). Results are available within a few hours. Patients should inform their doctor about all medications, as some (diuretics, aspirin) can affect results. The test is safe, inexpensive, and essential for diagnosing and managing gout and related conditions.',

            'URINE M/C/S' => 'Urine Culture and Sensitivity (M/C/S) is a laboratory test used to diagnose urinary tract infections (UTIs) by identifying the bacteria or fungi present in a urine sample and determining which antibiotics are effective against them. A midstream clean-catch urine specimen is collected in a sterile container. The sample is inoculated onto culture media and incubated; growth is identified and quantified. Colony counts of 10^5 CFU/mL or higher indicate significant bacteriuria. Sensitivity testing (antibiogram) is performed on isolated organisms to guide antibiotic choice. The test is crucial for managing recurrent UTIs, pyelonephritis, and infections in catheterized patients. It takes 48–72 hours for complete results. Patients should avoid antibiotics before collection to prevent false negatives. Proper collection technique is essential to avoid contamination. The test is non-invasive and safe. Results help reduce empirical broad-spectrum antibiotic use and combat antimicrobial resistance. Clinical correlation with symptoms is necessary to distinguish colonization from true infection.',

            'HVS M/C/S,ECS M/C/S' => 'The High Vaginal Swab (HVS) and Endocervical Swab (ECS) with Culture and Sensitivity are tests used to diagnose infections of the female genital tract. The HVS collects secretions from the vaginal wall to detect organisms causing vaginitis (e.g., Candida, Gardnerella, Trichomonas), while the ECS samples the endocervical canal to detect sexually transmitted pathogens such as Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis. Both samples are collected using sterile swabs during a pelvic examination, with minimal discomfort. The swabs are then sent to the laboratory for culture on selective media and sensitivity testing to guide antibiotic therapy. Results are usually available within 48–72 hours. These tests are essential for women presenting with vaginal discharge, pelvic pain, abnormal bleeding, or infertility. They also serve as screening tools in high-risk populations and during pregnancy to prevent neonatal complications. Proper collection technique is critical to avoid contamination. Patients should avoid douching or using vaginal creams before the test. Prompt diagnosis and treatment prevent ascending infections and long-term sequelae.',

            'SPERM ANALYSIS &CULTURE' => 'Semen Analysis and Culture is a comprehensive test to evaluate male fertility and diagnose infections of the reproductive tract. The analysis assesses semen volume, sperm count, motility, morphology, and the presence of white blood cells (indicative of infection). The culture component identifies any bacteria or fungi that may be causing prostatitis, epididymitis, or orchitis. The sample is obtained by masturbation into a sterile container after 2–5 days of abstinence. It should be delivered to the laboratory within 30–60 minutes. Normal parameters according to WHO guidelines include a sperm concentration of at least 15 million/mL, total motility ≥40%, and normal forms ≥4%. The culture is incubated for 48–72 hours to detect pathogenic organisms. This test is crucial for couples with infertility and for men with symptoms of genital infection. It also helps guide antibiotic treatment for bacterial infections. Patients should avoid alcohol and certain medications that may affect semen quality. The test is non-invasive but requires careful handling to ensure accurate results.',

            'BLOOD M/C/S' => 'Blood Culture and Sensitivity (M/C/S) is a test used to detect microorganisms (bacteria or fungi) in the blood, indicating bacteremia or sepsis. It is one of the most critical tests in critical care and infectious disease management. A blood sample is collected under strict aseptic conditions from a peripheral vein and inoculated into culture bottles containing growth media. The bottles are incubated in an automated system that monitors for microbial growth. If positive, the organism is identified and sensitivity testing is performed to determine the most effective antibiotics. The test is essential for diagnosing bloodstream infections, endocarditis, and pyrexia of unknown origin. It typically takes 24–72 hours for preliminary results, with full sensitivity after an additional 24–48 hours. Timing of collection is important: ideally before antibiotics are administered, and when fever spikes occur. Multiple samples may be needed. The test is safe but requires careful venipuncture. Positive results demand urgent clinical intervention. Negative results do not rule out infection and should be correlated with clinical signs.',

            'PREGNANCY SCAN' => 'The Pregnancy Scan, also known as an obstetric ultrasound, is a routine imaging test performed during pregnancy to assess fetal development, confirm gestational age, detect multiple pregnancies, and screen for structural abnormalities. It is typically done at various gestational ages: first trimester (dating scan), second trimester (anomaly scan at 18–22 weeks), and third trimester (growth scan). The scan uses high-frequency sound waves to produce real-time images of the fetus, placenta, amniotic fluid, and maternal pelvic organs. It is non-invasive and painless; a transducer is moved over the abdomen after applying a conductive gel. A full bladder is often required for early scans to improve visualization. The procedure takes about 20–30 minutes. It provides critical information about fetal heart rate, biometry, and anatomy. Parents can often see the baby\'s movements and hear the heartbeat. The scan is safe, with no known adverse effects on the mother or fetus. It helps in planning prenatal care and identifying high-risk pregnancies. Results are usually discussed immediately by the sonologist or obstetrician.',

            'PELVIC SCAN' => 'The Pelvic Scan is an ultrasound examination of the female pelvic organs, including the uterus, ovaries, fallopian tubes, and surrounding structures. It is used to evaluate pelvic pain, abnormal bleeding, infertility, and to monitor ovarian follicles during fertility treatment. The scan can be performed transabdominally (with a full bladder) or transvaginally (with an empty bladder) for better resolution. It is non-invasive, painless, and takes about 20 minutes. The scan can detect uterine fibroids, ovarian cysts, endometriosis, pelvic inflammatory disease, and congenital anomalies. It is also used to assess the endometrium in cases of postmenopausal bleeding. The transvaginal approach provides clearer images of the endometrium and ovaries. No special preparation is needed for transvaginal, but for transabdominal, the patient should drink water to fill the bladder. The scan is safe and does not involve radiation. Results are interpreted by a radiologist or gynecologist and help guide further investigations or treatment.',

            'ABDOMINAL SCAN' => 'The Abdominal Scan is an ultrasound examination of the upper abdomen, including the liver, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, kidneys, and major blood vessels. It is used to investigate abdominal pain, jaundice, abnormal liver function tests, suspected gallstones, kidney stones, and abdominal masses. The procedure requires fasting for 6–8 hours to reduce bowel gas and empty the gallbladder. The patient lies on their back while a transducer is moved over the abdomen with gel applied. The scan takes 20–30 minutes and is painless. It can detect fatty liver, cirrhosis, tumours, gallstones, pancreatitis, kidney cysts, and aortic aneurysms. It is also used to guide needle biopsies or fluid aspiration. The scan is safe, non-invasive, and widely available. Results are available within a few hours. It is often the first-line imaging modality for many abdominal complaints due to its safety and effectiveness.',

            'ABDOMINO-PELVIC SCAN' => 'The Abdomino-Pelvic Scan combines ultrasound imaging of both the upper abdomen and the pelvic region, providing a comprehensive evaluation of the liver, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, kidneys, bladder, uterus, prostate, and other structures. It is indicated for patients with diffuse abdominal symptoms, pelvic pain, or for routine check-ups. The procedure requires fasting for the abdominal portion and a full bladder for the pelvic part. The scan is performed transabdominally and takes about 45 minutes. It can detect gallstones, kidney stones, liver tumours, ovarian cysts, uterine fibroids, and prostate enlargement. It is also useful for assessing the renal tract in patients with haematuria. The test is safe and painless, with no radiation exposure. Results are interpreted by a radiologist and help guide management for many conditions. Patients should follow preparation instructions carefully to ensure optimal image quality.',

            'BREAST SCAN' => 'The Breast Scan, or breast ultrasound, is a non-invasive imaging test used to evaluate breast lumps, pain, or abnormal mammographic findings. It uses high-frequency sound waves to produce detailed images of breast tissue, distinguishing between solid masses (which may be benign or malignant) and fluid-filled cysts. It is often performed as a complementary test to mammography, especially in younger women with dense breast tissue. The procedure involves the patient lying on her back while a transducer is moved over the breast with gel. It is painless and takes about 15–20 minutes. The scan can guide fine-needle aspiration or core biopsy of suspicious lesions. It is also used to assess breast implants and to monitor benign lesions over time. The test is safe, does not use ionizing radiation, and is suitable for pregnant women. Results are available immediately for discussion, but final interpretation and correlation with other imaging may take longer.',

            'PROSTRATE SCAN' => 'The Prostate Scan, also known as transrectal ultrasound (TRUS), is an imaging test used to evaluate the prostate gland for conditions such as benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), prostatitis, or prostate cancer. A small ultrasound probe is inserted into the rectum to obtain high-resolution images of the prostate and seminal vesicles. The procedure may be slightly uncomfortable but is generally well-tolerated; a local anaesthetic gel is applied. It takes about 15–20 minutes. The scan measures the size of the prostate, detects hypoechoic lesions, and is used to guide needle biopsies in patients with elevated PSA (prostate-specific antigen) or abnormal digital rectal exams. It is also used to assess the volume of residual urine and to evaluate patients with voiding symptoms. The test is safe, but patients with bleeding disorders or recent rectal surgery should inform their doctor. Results are interpreted by a urologist and help guide management.',

            'THYRIOD SCAN' => 'The Thyroid Scan is an ultrasound examination of the thyroid gland to assess its size, shape, and structure. It is used to investigate thyroid nodules, goitre, neck swelling, or abnormal thyroid function tests. The test is non-invasive and involves placing a transducer on the neck after applying gel. It takes about 15 minutes. The scan can differentiate between solid and cystic nodules, detect calcifications, and guide fine-needle aspiration cytology (FNAC) for suspicious nodules. It also evaluates the surrounding lymph nodes and the parathyroid glands. The test is safe, painless, and uses no radiation. It is often the first-line imaging for thyroid pathology. Results are available immediately, but a definitive diagnosis often requires correlation with thyroid function tests and biopsy if indicated. The scan is essential for monitoring nodule growth over time.',

            'OCCULAR EYE SCAN' => 'The Ocular Eye Scan, or ophthalmic ultrasound (B-scan), is an imaging technique used to evaluate the posterior segment of the eye when the view is obscured by cataracts or other opacities. It is used to diagnose retinal detachment, vitreous haemorrhage, intraocular tumours, and foreign bodies. The procedure involves applying gel to the closed eyelid and placing a transducer on the lid; the patient looks in different directions. It is painless and takes about 10–15 minutes. The scan provides cross-sectional images of the vitreous, retina, choroid, and optic nerve. It is essential in emergency ophthalmology and pre-operative assessment for cataract surgery. The test is safe and does not involve radiation. Results are interpreted by an ophthalmologist. Patients may need to dilate their pupils for a comprehensive evaluation. The scan is particularly valuable when direct fundoscopy is not possible.',

            'SCROTAL SCAN' => 'The Scrotal Scan is a high-frequency ultrasound of the scrotum and its contents, including the testes, epididymis, and spermatic cord. It is used to evaluate scrotal pain, swelling, masses, infertility, and to rule out testicular torsion. The procedure involves applying gel to the scrotum and moving a transducer over it. It is painless and takes about 15–20 minutes. The scan can differentiate between benign hydroceles, varicoceles, epididymal cysts, and solid testicular tumours. It is the primary imaging tool for testicular cancer screening in at-risk patients. Doppler ultrasound can assess blood flow to detect torsion (absent flow) or inflammation (increased flow). The test is safe and non-invasive. Results are available immediately and are interpreted by a radiologist. Prompt diagnosis of torsion can save the testis, making this scan a critical emergency tool.',

            'TRANSRECTAL SCAN' => 'The Transrectal Scan (TRUS) is an ultrasound examination of the rectum and surrounding pelvic structures, most commonly used to evaluate the prostate gland. A lubricated ultrasound probe is gently inserted into the rectum to obtain high-resolution images of the prostate, seminal vesicles, and adjacent tissues. The procedure takes about 10–15 minutes and may cause mild discomfort. It is used to measure prostate size, detect suspicious areas, and guide prostate biopsy in patients with elevated PSA or abnormal digital rectal exam. It is also used to assess the bladder neck and to evaluate patients with hematospermia or chronic pelvic pain. The test is safe but requires preparation such as a cleansing enema and antibiotic prophylaxis to reduce infection risk. Results are interpreted by a urologist. TRUS-guided biopsy is the standard method for diagnosing prostate cancer. The test is essential for managing lower urinary tract symptoms.',

            'TRANSVIGINAL SCAN' => 'The Transvaginal Scan (TVS) is an ultrasound examination performed with a specially designed probe inserted into the vagina to obtain detailed images of the uterus, endometrium, ovaries, and fallopian tubes. It offers higher resolution than transabdominal scanning for pelvic structures and is used in early pregnancy assessment, evaluation of pelvic pain, abnormal bleeding, and infertility. The procedure is generally painless, though some women may feel mild pressure. It takes about 15–20 minutes and requires an empty bladder. It can detect uterine fibroids, polyps, ovarian cysts, endometrial thickness, and ectopic pregnancies. It is also used for follicle tracking in fertility treatment and for assessing pelvic masses. The scan is safe and does not use radiation. Results are interpreted by a gynecologist or sonologist. It is a key tool in women\'s health, allowing precise diagnosis and treatment planning.',

            'TRANSFONTANELLE SCAN' => 'The Transfontanelle Scan is a cranial ultrasound performed through the anterior fontanelle (the soft spot on a baby\'s skull) to evaluate the brain in infants. It is commonly used in neonates to detect intraventricular haemorrhage, periventricular leukomalacia, hydrocephalus, congenital malformations, and other brain abnormalities. The procedure is painless, uses a small transducer with gel on the fontanelle, and takes about 15 minutes. It is safe and does not involve radiation. The scan provides real-time images of the ventricles, brain parenchyma, and the vascular system. It is particularly useful in premature infants and those with birth asphyxia or sepsis. Results are interpreted by a pediatric radiologist or neonatologist. The test is non-invasive and can be performed at the bedside in the NICU. It is essential for early detection and management of neurological conditions in newborns.',

            'SOFT TISSUE SCAN' => 'The Soft Tissue Scan is an ultrasound examination used to evaluate masses, swellings, or injuries in the muscles, tendons, ligaments, joints, and subcutaneous tissues. It is commonly used to assess lumps, hematomas, abscesses, tendon tears, and foreign bodies. The procedure involves placing a transducer over the affected area with gel and moving it to obtain images in multiple planes. It is painless and takes about 15–20 minutes. The scan can differentiate between cystic and solid lesions, assess vascularity with Doppler, and guide aspiration or biopsy. It is often used in orthopedics, rheumatology, and sports medicine. The test is safe, non-invasive, and provides immediate information to guide treatment. Results are interpreted by a radiologist or musculoskeletal specialist. It is a valuable tool for diagnosing soft tissue disorders and monitoring response to therapy.',

            'CHEST SCAN' => 'The Chest Scan typically refers to a chest ultrasound or computed tomography (CT) scan of the chest. In the context of this price list, it likely refers to a chest ultrasound, which is used to evaluate pleural effusions, lung masses, chest wall lesions, and to guide thoracentesis. The test is performed by placing a transducer on the chest wall, often with the patient sitting or lying down. It is non-invasive, painless, and takes about 15–20 minutes. It can detect fluid collections, empyema, pneumothorax, and diaphragmatic motion. It is especially useful in critically ill patients for bedside assessment. Chest CT provides more detailed cross-sectional imaging for lung nodules, mediastinal masses, and interstitial lung disease. Both modalities are safe (ultrasound no radiation, CT uses low-dose radiation). The choice depends on clinical indication. Results are interpreted by a radiologist and are essential for respiratory and critical care.',

            'WAIST SCAN' => 'The Waist Scan is likely a non-standard term; it may refer to an ultrasound of the abdominal wall or flank region. In clinical practice, it could be used to evaluate hernias, lipomas, or other palpable lumps in the waist area. The scan is performed by applying gel and moving a transducer over the region. It is painless and takes about 10 minutes. It helps differentiate between solid masses, cysts, and hernias. It is often used in general practice for localized swelling. The test is safe and provides immediate results. If needed, it can be extended to assess deeper organs. The term may also colloquially refer to a measurement of waist circumference for metabolic risk assessment, but in the context of this price list, it is more likely an ultrasound scan. Patients should discuss with their clinician the specific indication for this scan.',

            'FOLLOCULAR/OVULATION TRACKING' => 'Follicular/Ovulation Tracking is a series of transvaginal ultrasounds performed during the menstrual cycle to monitor the development of ovarian follicles and predict ovulation. It is commonly used in fertility treatments and for women with irregular cycles. Starting from cycle day 8–10, serial scans are done every 2–3 days to measure follicle size and endometrial thickness. The dominant follicle typically grows 1–2 mm per day and reaches 18–24 mm before ovulation. The scan also assesses the endometrial pattern and detects signs of ovulation (collapse of follicle, free fluid). The procedure is painless but requires an empty bladder. It helps time intercourse, intrauterine insemination, or in-vitro fertilization. The number of scans per cycle may vary. The test is safe, non-invasive, and provides critical information for reproductive health. Results are discussed with a gynecologist to optimize conception chances.',

            'SERIAL FOLLOCULAR/OVULATION SCAN IN ONE MENSTRUAL CYCLE T.V.S' => 'This package includes a series of transvaginal scans for follicular monitoring throughout a single menstrual cycle, typically performed 3–4 times or more. The scans track follicle growth, endometrial development, and ovulation timing. The service is often used in fertility clinics to maximize chances of conception. Each scan provides detailed measurements and is scheduled according to the patient\'s cycle. The package is cost-effective compared to paying per scan individually. The procedure is the same as routine TVS: a probe is inserted gently, and images are captured. It is non-invasive, safe, and requires no special preparation other than an empty bladder. The results are used to guide the next steps in fertility treatment. This thorough monitoring helps identify any ovulation disorders and allows for timely interventions. Patients receive a report after each scan, and cumulative data aids in individualized care.',

            'H.S.G' => 'Hysterosalpingography (HSG) is a specialized X-ray examination used to evaluate the shape of the uterine cavity and the patency of the fallopian tubes. It involves injecting a contrast dye through the cervix and taking fluoroscopic images as the dye fills the uterus and spills into the tubes and peritoneal cavity. The test is primarily performed in women with infertility or recurrent miscarriage to detect uterine anomalies, intrauterine adhesions, fibroids, and tubal blockage. It is usually scheduled between days 5–12 of the menstrual cycle to avoid interfering with a possible early pregnancy. Patients may experience mild cramping during the procedure; premedication with NSAIDs may be given. The test takes about 15–30 minutes. It is safe but involves a small amount of radiation. Results are interpreted by a radiologist. HSG can also have a therapeutic effect by flushing the tubes, improving fertility. Alternative procedures like sonohysterography or laparoscopy may be used if needed.',

            'ECHO CARDIOGRAPHY' => 'Echocardiography (Echo) is a non-invasive ultrasound test that evaluates the structure and function of the heart. It uses sound waves to produce detailed images of the heart chambers, valves, and blood flow. The test is performed by placing a transducer on the chest (transthoracic echo) or, in some cases, down the esophagus (transesophageal echo). It is painless and takes about 30–60 minutes. Echo is used to diagnose heart failure, valvular diseases, congenital heart defects, and to assess cardiac function after a heart attack. Doppler ultrasound measures blood velocities and pressures within the cardiac chambers. The test provides critical information for cardiologists to plan treatments, including surgery. It is safe, with no radiation. Patients may be asked to remove clothing from the upper body and lie on their left side. Results are available immediately or within a few days after formal reporting. It is an essential tool in cardiology.',

            'ANTIMULLERIAN HORMONE TEST (FERT.)' => 'The Anti-Müllerian Hormone (AMH) test is a blood test used to assess a woman\'s ovarian reserve, i.e., the number of remaining eggs in the ovaries. It is a key biomarker in fertility evaluation, helping to predict a woman\'s response to ovarian stimulation during IVF and to estimate the remaining reproductive lifespan. AMH is produced by small growing follicles and remains relatively stable throughout the menstrual cycle, so the test can be done on any day. The test requires a simple blood draw; no fasting is needed. Results are typically available within 1–2 days. Low AMH levels indicate diminished ovarian reserve, which may be associated with difficulty conceiving. The test is often used alongside antral follicle count and FSH levels. It is also used in the management of polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), where AMH levels are often elevated. The test is safe and provides valuable information for reproductive planning.',

            'MP' => 'The Malaria Parasite (MP) test is a rapid and reliable method to detect Plasmodium species in the blood. It is the cornerstone of malaria diagnosis in endemic regions. The test is performed by examining a thin and thick blood smear under a microscope or by using rapid diagnostic tests (RDTs) that detect specific antigens (HRP2, pLDH). A small blood sample is obtained via finger prick or venipuncture. Results are available within 15–30 minutes for RDTs, or a few hours for microscopy. The test identifies the species and quantifies parasitemia, which is essential for guiding treatment. Early diagnosis and treatment reduce morbidity and mortality. The test is safe, inexpensive, and widely available. Patients with fever, chills, and sweating should undergo testing. False negatives may occur in low parasitemia or with improper technique. Therefore, the test should be repeated if suspicion remains high. The MP test is a vital tool in malaria control programs.',

            'WIDAL' => 'The Widal test is a serological assay used to detect antibodies against Salmonella typhi and Salmonella paratyphi, the bacteria responsible for typhoid fever. It is commonly used in developing countries where enteric fever is endemic. The test measures agglutinating antibodies (O and H antigens) in the patient\'s serum. A fourfold rise in titre between acute and convalescent samples is diagnostic, but single titres are often interpreted based on local cutoff values. The test is performed on a venous blood sample; no fasting is required. Results are available within 24–48 hours. Limitations include cross-reactivity with other infections and background titres in endemic areas, so it should be interpreted with clinical correlation. The test is useful for monitoring response to treatment. It is a simple, cost-effective test but is being replaced by more specific PCR and blood culture methods. Patients with suspected typhoid should have blood cultures performed as well.',

            'HVS M/C/S' => 'High Vaginal Swab Culture and Sensitivity is a test to identify organisms causing vaginitis and to determine antibiotic susceptibility. A sterile swab is used to collect discharge from the vaginal vault. The swab is then cultured on selective media to detect Candida species, Gardnerella vaginalis, Trichomonas vaginalis, and other bacteria. Sensitivity testing is performed to choose appropriate antimicrobials. The test is indicated for women with vaginal discharge, itching, odour, or pelvic pain. It is also part of routine sexually transmitted infection screening. Proper collection technique is essential; patients should avoid douching and intercourse for 24 hours prior. Results are usually available in 48–72 hours. The test guides targeted therapy and reduces unnecessary use of broad-spectrum antibiotics. It is safe and well-tolerated. Patients should be counseled on the results and treatment options. This test is an integral part of women\'s health services.',

            'SEMENAL FLUID M/C/S' => 'Seminal Fluid Culture and Sensitivity is a test to detect infections in the male reproductive tract that may affect fertility or cause symptoms such as pain or discharge. A semen sample is collected by masturbation after 2–5 days of abstinence. It is then cultured on media to isolate bacteria, fungi, or mycoplasma. Positive cultures are followed by antibiotic sensitivity testing. The test is often performed alongside semen analysis. It is essential for diagnosing prostatitis, epididymitis, and seminal vesiculitis. Patients should provide a sample in a sterile container and deliver it to the lab within an hour. Results take 48–72 hours. The test helps in tailoring antimicrobial therapy and preventing recurrent infections. It is non-invasive but requires patient cooperation. Clinical correlation with symptoms and physical examination is important to interpret results. The test is safe and widely available.',

            'SEMINAL FLUID ANALYSIS' => 'Seminal Fluid Analysis (SFA) is a comprehensive evaluation of a semen sample to assess male fertility. It measures sperm count (concentration), total motility, progressive motility, morphology (shape), and the presence of white blood cells or agglutination. The analysis also includes pH, volume, liquefaction time, and viscosity. The test is performed on a sample obtained by masturbation after 2–5 days of abstinence. It should be examined within 30–60 minutes of collection. Normal values are set by the WHO (e.g., concentration ≥15 million/mL, motility ≥40%, morphology ≥4% normal forms). Abnormal results may indicate conditions such as varicocele, infection, hormonal imbalance, or obstruction. The test is the cornerstone of infertility evaluation for men. It is non-invasive but requires proper collection and timely analysis. Results guide further investigations like hormonal assays, genetic testing, or imaging. It is safe and provides critical information for fertility management.',

            'BLOOD CULTURE' => 'Blood Culture is a test to detect bacteremia or fungemia (presence of microorganisms in the blood). It is essential for diagnosing sepsis, endocarditis, and other serious infections. A sample of blood is drawn aseptically from a peripheral vein and inoculated into aerobic and anaerobic culture bottles. The bottles are incubated in an automated system that monitors for growth. If positive, the organism is identified and antibiotic sensitivity testing is performed. Results are typically available within 24–48 hours for preliminary positivity, and full sensitivity after 72 hours. The test requires careful collection to avoid contamination; skin disinfection is crucial. Multiple sets may be drawn from different sites. Patients with fever, chills, or hypotension should have blood cultures before starting antibiotics. The test is safe but may cause minor bruising. Positive results require immediate clinical intervention. Negative results do not exclude infection, especially if antibiotics were given prior.',

            'WOUND SWAB M/C/S' => 'Wound Swab Culture and Sensitivity is a test used to identify microorganisms infecting a wound and to determine effective antibiotics. It is performed on open wounds, surgical incisions, burns, or ulcers. A sterile swab is rotated over the wound bed to collect exudate or tissue. The swab is then cultured on appropriate media. Results include identification of the pathogen and an antibiogram. The test is essential for managing chronic wounds, diabetic foot infections, and post-operative wound infections. It helps guide antibiotic choice and reduces the risk of antimicrobial resistance. Proper collection technique involves cleaning the wound and sampling from the deepest part. Results take 48–72 hours. The test is safe and painless. Clinical correlation with wound appearance and patient risk factors is necessary. This test is a routine part of wound care and infectious disease management.',

            'LIVER FUNCTION TEST (LFT)' => 'Liver Function Tests (LFTs) are a group of blood tests that assess the health of the liver and its function. They measure enzymes (ALT, AST, ALP, GGT), proteins (albumin, total protein), and bilirubin levels. These markers can indicate hepatocellular injury, cholestasis, or synthetic dysfunction. LFTs are used to screen for liver disease, monitor drug-induced liver injury, and evaluate jaundice or right upper quadrant pain. The test requires a venous blood sample; fasting is usually not needed but may be recommended for certain measures. Results are available within a few hours to 1 day. Abnormal results require further investigation, such as viral hepatitis serology, ultrasound, or biopsy. The test is safe and widely available. It is essential for managing chronic liver conditions and monitoring treatment response. Clinical interpretation must consider the patient\'s history and other laboratory findings.',

            'KIDNEY FUNCTION TEST (S/E/U/Cr)' => 'Kidney Function Tests, commonly comprising serum electrolytes (sodium, potassium, chloride, bicarbonate), urea, and creatinine, evaluate how well the kidneys are filtering waste products from the blood. These tests are essential for diagnosing acute kidney injury, chronic kidney disease, and electrolyte imbalances. The test requires a venous blood sample; fasting is not required but may be recommended for urea measurement. Results are available within a few hours. Elevated creatinine and urea indicate reduced glomerular filtration rate, while abnormal electrolytes point to acid-base or fluid disorders. The test is used to monitor patients with hypertension, diabetes, and those on nephrotoxic drugs. It helps in staging CKD and adjusting medication doses. The test is safe and inexpensive. Clinical correlation with urine output and imaging (renal ultrasound) is often needed. This panel is a routine component of health check-ups.',

            'VDRL' => 'VDRL (Venereal Disease Research Laboratory) is a screening test for syphilis, a sexually transmitted infection caused by Treponema pallidum. It is a non-treponemal test that detects antibodies to cardiolipin, which are produced during the infection. The test is performed on blood serum; no fasting is required. Results are reported as reactive or non-reactive, and titres can be quantified to monitor response to treatment. The test is sensitive but not specific; false positives can occur in pregnancy, autoimmune diseases, and other infections. Positive results must be confirmed with a treponemal test like FTA-ABS or TPHA. The test is used for routine screening in high-risk populations and during pregnancy. It is safe and inexpensive. Treatment with penicillin is effective if initiated early. The test is part of comprehensive sexually transmitted infection screening panels.',

            'HBsAg' => 'Hepatitis B Surface Antigen (HBsAg) is a blood test that detects the presence of the surface protein of the hepatitis B virus, indicating active infection. It is used for screening and diagnosis of hepatitis B. A positive result means the person is currently infected and can transmit the virus to others. The test requires a venous blood sample; no fasting is needed. Results are usually available within 1–2 days. HBsAg is often part of a hepatitis panel and is used in prenatal screening, pre-employment checks, and for blood donor screening. Chronic carriers require follow-up with liver function tests and viral load monitoring. The test is safe and widely available. It helps in identifying individuals who need vaccination (if negative) or antiviral therapy (if positive). Clinical management includes liver ultrasound and specialist referral. The test is essential for public health control of hepatitis B.',

            'HCV' => 'Hepatitis C Virus (HCV) antibody test detects the presence of antibodies to the hepatitis C virus, indicating past or current infection. A positive result requires further testing with HCV RNA to confirm active infection. The test is performed on a blood sample; no fasting is required. Results are available within 1–2 days. HCV is a major cause of chronic liver disease, cirrhosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma. The test is recommended for individuals at risk, such as those with history of injection drug use, blood transfusion before 1992, or HIV co-infection. The test is safe and inexpensive. If confirmed, direct-acting antiviral therapy can cure most cases. Screening is crucial for early treatment and prevention of transmission. The test is often included in comprehensive health panels.',

            'HEPATITIS \'B\' PROFILE' => 'The Hepatitis B Profile is a panel of serological markers that provides comprehensive information about a patient\'s hepatitis B status. It typically includes HBsAg (surface antigen), anti-HBs (surface antibody), anti-HBc (core antibody), HBeAg (e-antigen), and anti-HBe (e-antibody). This profile distinguishes between acute, chronic, resolved, and immune states, and helps in making vaccination decisions. It is performed on a venous blood sample; no fasting is required. Results are available within 1–2 days. The profile is used in screening for blood donors, pregnant women, and healthcare workers, and in evaluating patients with elevated liver enzymes. It guides treatment options for chronic hepatitis B and monitors response to antiviral therapy. The test is safe and widely available. Interpretation requires clinical correlation with liver function and viral load. It is a cornerstone of hepatology practice.',

            'PCV' => 'Packed Cell Volume (PCV), also known as haematocrit, measures the proportion of red blood cells in blood. It is a simple, quick test for anaemia and polycythaemia. A blood sample is drawn into a capillary tube, centrifuged, and the percentage of red cells is measured. Normal ranges: males 40–52%, females 36–48%. Low PCV indicates anaemia due to iron deficiency, blood loss, or bone marrow failure. High PCV may indicate dehydration, polycythaemia vera, or living at high altitude. The test is often performed alongside haemoglobin measurement. It requires minimal preparation and results are available within an hour. It is cost-effective and widely used in primary care. Patients with abnormal results should have a full blood count and further evaluation. The test is safe and reliable.',

            'GENOTYPE' => 'Genotype testing, in the context of haemoglobinopathies, determines the type of haemoglobin a person has, particularly to detect sickle cell trait or disease. Common genotypes include AA (normal), AS (sickle cell trait), and SS (sickle cell disease). The test is performed on a blood sample, often using haemoglobin electrophoresis or high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC). It is essential for genetic counselling, prenatal screening, and management of sickle cell disease. The test requires a venous blood draw; no fasting is needed. Results are available within 1–2 days. Knowledge of genotype is important for family planning and to prevent sickle cell crises. The test is safe and widely available. Patients with sickle cell disease require regular follow-up and multidisciplinary care. The test is often done in newborns as part of routine screening in some regions.',

            'BLOOD GROUP' => 'Blood Group testing determines the ABO blood type (A, B, AB, O) and RhD factor (positive or negative). It is performed on a blood sample by mixing red cells with anti-A, anti-B, and anti-D antibodies. The test is essential for blood transfusions, organ transplantation, and pregnancy management to prevent Rh incompatibility. It is also used in forensic and paternity testing. The procedure is simple and rapid, with results available within minutes. No special preparation is required. The test is safe and low-cost. It is performed on all patients before any transfusion. In pregnancy, Rh-negative women may receive anti-D immunoglobulin to prevent sensitization. The test is a fundamental part of blood bank services and emergency medicine.',

            'PREGNANCY TEST (BLOOD/URINE)' => 'Pregnancy tests detect the presence of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), a hormone produced by the placenta after implantation. Urine tests (dipstick or cassette) are qualitative and used for home or clinic use, while blood tests (quantitative serum hCG) can detect very low levels and monitor progression. The blood test requires a venous sample; the urine test requires a sample in a container. No fasting is needed. Blood tests are more sensitive and can detect pregnancy as early as 7–10 days after ovulation. Results for urine are available in 5–10 minutes, while blood results may take a few hours. The test is used for confirmation of pregnancy, ruling out ectopic pregnancy, and monitoring after miscarriage or fertility treatment. It is safe, non-invasive, and widely available. Positive results should be confirmed with ultrasound for dating and viability.',

            'CHOLESTEROL (LIPID PROFILE)' => 'The Lipid Profile measures total cholesterol, LDL, HDL, and triglycerides. It is used to assess cardiovascular risk. The test requires a blood sample after 9–12 hours of fasting. Results are available within 24 hours. Optimal values: total cholesterol <5.2 mmol/L, LDL <2.6 mmol/L (or <1.8 for high-risk), HDL >1.0 mmol/L (men) or >1.3 mmol/L (women), triglycerides <1.7 mmol/L. Elevated levels increase the risk of atherosclerosis, heart attack, and stroke. The test is recommended for adults every 4–6 years. Lifestyle changes and statins can improve levels. The test is safe and inexpensive. Interpretation should consider age, gender, and other risk factors. It is an essential component of health screening.',

            'FASTING BLOOD SUGAR' => 'Fasting Blood Sugar (FBS) measures glucose after an overnight fast of 8–12 hours. It is the primary test for diabetes and prediabetes. A venous blood sample is drawn. Normal <5.6 mmol/L (100 mg/dL); prediabetes 5.6–6.9 mmol/L; diabetes ≥7.0 mmol/L on two occasions. The test is simple, rapid, and safe. Patients are advised to fast and avoid strenuous exercise. Results are available within hours. The test is used for screening, diagnosis, and monitoring of diabetes. It is essential for managing metabolic syndrome and preventing complications. Clinical correlation with HbA1c and oral glucose tolerance test may be needed. The test is cost-effective and widely available.',

            'H. PYLORI' => 'Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) testing detects the presence of this bacterium in the stomach, which is a major cause of chronic gastritis, peptic ulcer disease, and gastric cancer. Tests include stool antigen, urea breath test, and serology (antibodies). The stool antigen test detects active infection; the breath test requires ingestion of a labelled urea and measurement of exhaled carbon dioxide; serology detects past or present infection but cannot differentiate. The test is non-invasive; stool or breath samples are collected. No special preparation may be needed, but antibiotics and proton pump inhibitors should be stopped before testing. Results are available within hours to days. Positive results indicate the need for eradication therapy with antibiotics and acid suppression. The test is safe and widely used in gastroenterology. Eradication reduces ulcer recurrence and cancer risk.',

            'FASTING LIPID PROFILE' => 'Fasting Lipid Profile is a blood test that measures total cholesterol, LDL, HDL, and triglycerides after a 9–12 hour fast. It provides accurate assessment of cardiovascular risk. The procedure is a simple venipuncture. Results are available within 24 hours. Optimal levels are as above. The test is used to screen for dyslipidemia, monitor lipid-lowering therapy, and assess overall cardiovascular health. It is recommended for adults with risk factors such as diabetes, hypertension, smoking, or family history. The test is safe and inexpensive. Patients should avoid alcohol and high-fat foods before the test. Clinical decisions regarding statin therapy rely heavily on these values. The test is a cornerstone of preventive medicine.',

            'CD4 COUNT' => 'CD4 Count measures the number of CD4+ T lymphocytes per microlitre of blood. It is used primarily to monitor the immune status of HIV-infected individuals and to guide treatment decisions. A low CD4 count (<200 cells/µL) indicates severe immunosuppression and risk of opportunistic infections. The test requires a venous blood sample and is often performed using flow cytometry. No fasting is needed. Results are available within 1–2 days. The test is essential for staging HIV infection, deciding when to initiate antiretroviral therapy, and monitoring response to treatment. It is also used in other conditions affecting immunity. The test is safe but requires specialized equipment. Regular monitoring is crucial for people living with HIV. The CD4 count helps guide prophylaxis and assess prognosis.',

            'HORMONE ANALYSIS' => 'Hormone Analysis refers to a panel of tests measuring various hormones such as thyroid hormones (TSH, T3, T4), adrenal hormones (cortisol, DHEA), sex hormones (estradiol, testosterone, progesterone), and pituitary hormones (FSH, LH). These tests are used to diagnose endocrine disorders, infertility, and menstrual irregularities. The test requires a blood sample, often at specific times or phases of the menstrual cycle. Fasting may not be required, but timing is crucial. Results are available within a few days. The panel is comprehensive but can be tailored. It helps identify hormonal imbalances and guide treatment. The test is safe and widely available. Interpretation requires consideration of age, gender, and clinical context. Hormone analysis is fundamental in endocrinology and reproductive medicine.',

            'SERUM PROLACTIN' => 'Serum Prolactin measures the level of prolactin, a hormone produced by the pituitary gland that stimulates milk production. Elevated levels (hyperprolactinemia) can cause galactorrhea, menstrual disturbances, and infertility. The test is performed on a venous blood sample, ideally taken in the morning after fasting and avoiding stress and breast stimulation. Results are available within 1–2 days. Normal ranges vary by sex and laboratory. High levels may indicate a prolactinoma or other pituitary disorders. The test is used to evaluate galactorrhea, amenorrhea, and erectile dysfunction. It is also monitored in patients on antipsychotic medications. Treatment includes dopamine agonists. The test is safe and non-invasive. Clinical correlation with imaging (MRI) may be needed.',

            'SERUM TB (TUBERCULOSIS)' => 'Serum TB (Tuberculosis) tests, such as QuantiFERON-TB Gold or T-SPOT.TB, are interferon-gamma release assays (IGRAs) used to detect latent tuberculosis infection. They are more specific than the tuberculin skin test. The test requires a blood sample, and results are available within 24 hours. A positive result indicates infection with M. tuberculosis, but does not differentiate between latent and active disease. The test is used for screening high-risk individuals, including contacts of TB patients, healthcare workers, and immunocompromised patients. It is safe and does not require a follow-up visit for reading. It is not affected by BCG vaccination. Positive results require further evaluation with chest X-ray and clinical assessment. The test is increasingly used in TB control programs.',

            'MANTOUX TEST' => 'The Mantoux test, also known as the tuberculin skin test (TST), is used to screen for tuberculosis infection. It involves injecting a small amount of purified protein derivative (PPD) intradermally on the forearm and measuring the induration after 48–72 hours. A positive result indicates exposure to TB, but may be false-positive in BCG-vaccinated individuals. The test is used in contact tracing, pre-employment screening, and in settings with high TB incidence. It is safe but requires a return visit for reading. Interpretation depends on the patient\'s risk group. A positive result warrants further investigation with chest X-ray and sputum tests. The test is inexpensive and widely used in public health.',

            'URETHRAL SWAB M/C/S' => 'Urethral Swab Culture and Sensitivity is a test to diagnose sexually transmitted infections (STIs) such as gonorrhea and chlamydia in men. A sterile swab is inserted 1–2 cm into the urethra and rotated to collect epithelial cells. The sample is cultured on selective media; sensitivity testing is performed for positive cultures. The procedure may cause mild discomfort. Patients are advised to avoid urinating for 1–2 hours before the test. Results take 48–72 hours. The test is essential for diagnosing urethritis, guiding treatment, and preventing complications. It is often part of STI screening panels. The test is safe and widely available. Clinical correlation with symptoms (discharge, dysuria) is important.',

            'SPUTUM M/C/S' => 'Sputum Culture and Sensitivity is used to identify pathogenic organisms causing lower respiratory tract infections, such as pneumonia, bronchitis, or tuberculosis. Patients are instructed to cough deeply and produce sputum into a sterile container. The sample is then cultured on media to isolate bacteria or fungi. Sensitivity testing guides antibiotic selection. The test is essential for managing respiratory infections, especially in immunocompromised patients. Results take 48–72 hours for routine cultures, longer for TB. Proper collection is critical; saliva is not acceptable. The test is safe and non-invasive. It is often repeated if initial cultures are negative. Clinical correlation with chest X-ray and blood tests is necessary.',

            'SKIN SNIP' => 'The Skin Snip is a diagnostic test for onchocerciasis (river blindness) caused by the filarial worm Onchocerca volvulus. A small piece of skin (snip) is taken from the iliac crest or other sites using a sterile scalpel and examined microscopically for microfilariae. The procedure is performed under local anaesthesia and is generally well-tolerated. Results are available within a few hours. The test is used in endemic regions for diagnosis and epidemiological surveys. It is safe but may cause minor bleeding or scarring. Alternative tests include serology and PCR. A positive result indicates active infection and the need for ivermectin treatment. The test is essential for control programs.',

            'HEPATITIS \'B\' VIRAL LOAD TEST' => 'Hepatitis B Viral Load (HBV DNA) quantifies the amount of virus in the blood, indicating active replication and infectivity. It is used to monitor chronic hepatitis B, guide initiation of antiviral therapy, and assess treatment response. The test requires a blood sample; no fasting is needed. Results are available within 1–2 days and are reported in IU/mL. High viral loads (>2000 IU/mL) with elevated ALT may indicate treatment need. The test is essential for managing patients with positive HBsAg. It helps determine the risk of cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. The test is safe and widely available. Regular monitoring is recommended for chronic carriers. Clinical decisions should incorporate liver function tests and imaging.',

            'HEPATITIS \'C\' VIRAL LOAD TEST' => 'Hepatitis C Viral Load (HCV RNA) measures the amount of hepatitis C virus in the blood, confirming active infection and guiding treatment. The test is performed on a blood sample; no fasting is required. Results are reported in IU/mL. A detectable viral load indicates chronic infection; the test is used before starting antiviral therapy to determine genotype and baseline level. It is also used to monitor treatment response (sustained virologic response). The test is safe and highly specific. Modern direct-acting antivirals can cure the infection in most cases. The test is essential for managing hepatitis C. Clinical correlation with liver function and fibrosis assessment is needed. It is a key tool in hepatitis C elimination programs.',

            'BLOOD GROUP & CROSS MATCHING' => 'Blood Group testing determines the ABO and Rh type, while Cross-matching is a pre-transfusion test that mixes the donor\'s red cells with the recipient\'s serum to check for incompatibility. This ensures safe transfusion. The tests are performed on blood samples from both donor and recipient. Results are available within minutes to hours. They are essential before any blood transfusion, organ transplant, or in pregnancy. The test is safe and standardized. Cross-matching includes major (recipient serum + donor cells) and minor (donor serum + recipient cells) crossmatches. In emergency situations, type O negative blood may be used without crossmatch. The test is fundamental in blood banking and transfusion medicine.',

            'RHEUMATOID FACTOR' => 'Rheumatoid Factor (RF) is an autoantibody test used to help diagnose rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and other autoimmune conditions. It is performed on a blood sample; no fasting is needed. Results are reported as positive or negative, with titres. Elevated RF is found in about 70–80% of RA patients, but also in other diseases (Sjögren\'s syndrome, lupus) and in some healthy individuals (especially elderly). Therefore, a positive result is not diagnostic alone. The test is often combined with anti-CCP antibody for better specificity. It is used for initial evaluation of joint pain and swelling. The test is safe and inexpensive. Clinical correlation with symptoms and imaging is essential. Serial testing may monitor disease activity.',

            'MEDICAL FITNESS TEST WITH COMPUTER' => 'This term likely refers to a computer-based medical fitness assessment, which may include a battery of tests such as ECG, spirometry, vision, hearing, and blood pressure, integrated into a digital platform. It is used for pre-employment, sports, or insurance purposes. The test is non-invasive and may include multiple stations. Results are generated immediately. It provides a comprehensive health snapshot. The test is safe and efficient. It may include a physician consultation for interpretation. Specific components vary by package. Patients should wear comfortable clothing and bring relevant medical history. The test is designed to detect early signs of disease and ensure fitness for specific activities. It is increasingly used in corporate health programs.',

            'ESR' => 'The Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate (ESR) is a simple blood test that measures the rate at which red blood cells settle in a tube over one hour. It is a non-specific marker of inflammation. The test is performed by placing a blood sample in a Westergren tube and measuring the fall of red cells. Elevated ESR can indicate infection, autoimmune diseases, malignancies, or tissue injury. Normal values vary by age and gender. The test is often used to monitor disease activity in conditions like rheumatoid arthritis or giant cell arteritis. It requires a venous blood sample; no fasting is needed. Results are available within 1–2 hours. The test is safe and inexpensive. It is often combined with CRP for more specific inflammation assessment. ESR is not diagnostic alone, but helpful for monitoring.',

            'FOBT' => 'Faecal Occult Blood Test (FOBT) detects hidden (occult) blood in the stool, which may be a sign of colorectal cancer, polyps, or gastrointestinal bleeding. The test is performed by applying a small amount of stool to a test card and adding a developer solution. A positive result indicates the presence of blood, requiring further investigation with colonoscopy. Patients are advised to avoid red meat, certain vegetables (e.g., broccoli), and vitamin C supplements for 2–3 days before the test. The test is non-invasive and can be done at home. It is recommended annually for adults over 50. The test is safe and cost-effective. Positive results require prompt follow-up. It is a key component of colorectal cancer screening programs.',

            'STOOL M/C/S' => 'Stool Culture and Sensitivity is a test to identify pathogenic bacteria, viruses, or parasites causing gastrointestinal infections. A stool sample is collected in a sterile container and cultured on selective media. Sensitivity testing is performed for bacterial pathogens. The test is indicated for persistent diarrhea, bloody stool, or suspected foodborne illness. Results take 48–72 hours. Proper collection is important; the sample should not be contaminated with urine or water. The test is safe and non-invasive. It helps guide antimicrobial therapy and identify outbreaks. Clinical correlation with patient history and travel is essential. The test is a standard part of infectious gastroenteritis workup.',

            'HERPES SIMPLEX VIRUS 1' => 'Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1 (HSV-1) test detects antibodies or viral DNA to confirm infection with HSV-1, which typically causes oral herpes (cold sores) but can also cause genital herpes. The test is performed on blood (serology) or from a lesion swab (PCR). Serology detects IgG and IgM antibodies, indicating past or recent infection. PCR is more sensitive for active lesions. The test is used in patients with suspicious lesions, immunocompromised individuals, and for prenatal screening. No fasting is required. Results are available within 1–2 days for serology, a few hours for PCR. The test is safe and helps differentiate HSV-1 from HSV-2. Antiviral therapy can manage outbreaks. The test is part of comprehensive STI screening.',

            'HERPES SIMPLEX VIRUS 2' => 'Herpes Simplex Virus Type 2 (HSV-2) test is similar to HSV-1 but specifically detects antibodies or DNA for HSV-2, which is the primary cause of genital herpes. The test is used for diagnosis of genital ulcerative disease and for screening in high-risk populations. It is performed on blood (serology) or swab from a lesion. Serology can differentiate between HSV-1 and HSV-2 using type-specific glycoprotein G. The test is safe and reliable. Positive results indicate infection; antiviral therapy is effective in reducing symptoms and transmission. The test is often recommended for individuals with multiple sexual partners. Results are available within 1–2 days. It is an important tool in sexual health clinics.',

            'CANDIDA TEST' => 'Candida test detects the presence of Candida species, which are common causes of fungal infections such as oral thrush, vaginal yeast infections, and invasive candidiasis. Tests include microscopy, culture, and antigen detection (e.g., mannan). A sample is collected from the affected site (swab, blood, urine) and examined. The test is used in patients with recurrent or severe infections, immunocompromised individuals, and those with unexplained sepsis. Results are available in 1–3 days for culture. Sensitivity testing may be performed. The test is safe and non-invasive for superficial samples. Treatment includes antifungal medications. The test is essential for appropriate antifungal therapy and preventing resistance.',

            'CHLAMYDIAL TEST' => 'Chlamydial test detects Chlamydia trachomatis, the most common bacterial sexually transmitted infection. Testing is performed on urine, vaginal swab, cervical swab, or urethral swab using nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs), which are highly sensitive and specific. The test is used for screening asymptomatic individuals, diagnosing symptomatic infection, and post-treatment follow-up. No special preparation is needed, but patients should avoid urination for 1–2 hours before sampling. Results are available within 1–2 days. The test is safe and non-invasive. Positive results require treatment with antibiotics and partner notification. Untreated chlamydia can cause pelvic inflammatory disease and infertility. The test is a standard part of STI screening.',

            'T. VAGINALIS' => 'Trichomonas vaginalis (T. vaginalis) testing detects the protozoan parasite that causes trichomoniasis, a common sexually transmitted infection. Testing is performed on vaginal swab (for women) or urethral swab/urine (for men) using wet mount microscopy, culture, or NAAT. Wet mount is rapid but less sensitive; NAAT is more sensitive. The test is indicated for patients with vaginal discharge, itching, or dysuria. It is also part of STI screening panels. Results are available within minutes (wet mount) to days (culture). The test is safe and inexpensive. Treatment with metronidazole or tinidazole is effective. Partner treatment is important to prevent reinfection. The test is essential for women\'s health and STI management.',

            'RUBELLA' => 'Rubella testing detects antibodies (IgG and IgM) to the rubella virus, which causes German measles. It is primarily used to determine immunity in pregnant women and to diagnose acute infection. IgG indicates immunity from vaccination or past infection; IgM indicates recent infection. The test requires a blood sample; no fasting is needed. Results are available within 1–2 days. Rubella infection during pregnancy can cause congenital rubella syndrome, with severe birth defects. Therefore, immunity testing is routine in antenatal care. Non-immune women are offered vaccination post-partum. The test is safe and widely available. It is essential for maternal and child health.',

            'CYTOMEGALOVIRUS' => 'Cytomegalovirus (CMV) testing detects antibodies (IgG and IgM) or viral DNA to identify past or active CMV infection. CMV is a common herpesvirus that can cause serious disease in immunocompromised patients and congenitally infected newborns. IgG indicates past infection, while IgM suggests recent or active infection. PCR can detect viral DNA in blood, urine, or other fluids. The test is used in pregnancy screening, organ transplant recipients, and patients with mononucleosis-like illness. Results are available within 1–2 days. The test is safe and non-invasive. Management includes antiviral therapy in high-risk groups. The test is important for monitoring and prevention of CMV disease.',

            'IMMUNOGLOBULINS A, D, E, G, M' => 'Immunoglobulins (IgG, IgA, IgM, IgE, IgD) are antibodies that play key roles in the immune response. Measuring their levels helps diagnose immunodeficiency, autoimmune diseases, and allergic conditions. The test is performed on a blood sample; no fasting is needed. Results are available within 1–2 days. IgG is the most abundant, providing long-term immunity; IgA protects mucosal surfaces; IgM is produced in primary immune response; IgE is associated with allergy and parasitic infections; IgD function is less well-defined. Abnormal levels may indicate multiple myeloma, chronic infections, or primary immunodeficiencies. The test is safe and widely available. Interpretation requires clinical correlation. It is essential for immunology workup.',

            'TROPONIN KINASE' => 'This term is likely a misnomer; it may refer to Troponin I or T and Creatine Kinase (CK) or CK-MB. Troponin is a specific marker for myocardial injury (heart attack), while CK-MB is less specific. Both tests are used in the diagnosis of acute coronary syndrome. Troponin rises within 3–4 hours of myocardial infarction and remains elevated for days. The test requires a blood sample; no fasting is needed. Results are available within 1–2 hours. Elevated troponin indicates cardiac damage; serial measurements are used to monitor progression. The test is essential in emergency cardiology. It is safe and highly sensitive. High-sensitivity troponin assays are now standard. Interpretation must consider renal function and other causes of troponin elevation.',

            'CREATININE' => 'Creatinine is a waste product generated by muscle metabolism and excreted by the kidneys. Serum creatinine is a key marker of renal function. A venous blood sample is taken; no fasting is required. Normal levels are typically 0.6–1.2 mg/dL (53–106 µmol/L), varying by muscle mass. Elevated creatinine indicates decreased glomerular filtration rate (GFR), suggesting acute or chronic kidney disease. The test is used to estimate GFR using equations (e.g., CKD-EPI). It is essential for monitoring patients with hypertension, diabetes, and those on nephrotoxic drugs. The test is safe and inexpensive. Regular monitoring is crucial for early intervention in kidney disease. Clinical decisions often use creatinine trends.',

            'MYOGLOBIN' => 'Myoglobin is a protein found in cardiac and skeletal muscle; its presence in blood indicates muscle injury. It is used in the early diagnosis of myocardial infarction, but is less specific than troponin. It rises within 1–3 hours of injury and returns to normal within 24 hours. The test requires a blood sample; no fasting is needed. Results are available within 1–2 hours. Myoglobin is also elevated in rhabdomyolysis and severe muscle trauma. It is not commonly used alone; it is often paired with troponin for early detection. The test is safe and inexpensive. Its rapid rise makes it useful for early rule-out of MI, but troponin is preferred due to better specificity.',

            'C-REACTIVE PROTEIN' => 'C-Reactive Protein (CRP) is an acute-phase protein produced by the liver in response to inflammation. It is a sensitive but non-specific marker of infection, autoimmune disease, and tissue injury. High-sensitivity CRP (hs-CRP) is used to assess cardiovascular risk. The test requires a blood sample; no fasting is needed. Results are available within a few hours. Normal CRP is <10 mg/L; elevated levels indicate inflammation. The test is used to monitor disease activity in conditions like rheumatoid arthritis and to detect post-operative infections. It is also used in suspected sepsis. CRP is safe and widely available. It is often measured along with ESR. Clinical correlation is essential because CRP can be elevated in many conditions.',

            'GONORRHEA' => 'Gonorrhea testing detects Neisseria gonorrhoeae, a bacterial sexually transmitted infection. Testing is performed on urine, vaginal, cervical, urethral, or rectal swabs using NAAT, which is highly sensitive. It is used for screening asymptomatic individuals and diagnosing symptomatic infection. The test is often combined with chlamydia testing. No special preparation is needed, but avoid urination for 1–2 hours before urine samples. Results are available within 1–2 days. The test is safe and non-invasive. Positive results require antibiotic treatment (usually dual therapy) and partner notification. Untreated gonorrhea can cause pelvic inflammatory disease, infertility, and disseminated infection. The test is essential for STI control.',

            'SINGLE FOLDER' => 'A Single Folder is a physical medical record folder used to store an individual patient\'s health documents, test results, prescriptions, and clinical notes. It is designed to keep patient information organized, secure, and easily accessible during consultations and hospital visits. The folder is typically made of durable cardboard or plastic with pockets and dividers. It helps healthcare providers track a patient\'s medical history, ensuring continuity of care. The folder includes labels for patient identification and may have sections for different specialties. It is an essential administrative tool in any healthcare facility. Proper filing ensures quick retrieval of records, which improves clinical workflow and reduces errors. The folder is reusable and can be updated with new documents over time. It is a cost-effective solution for maintaining paper-based records. In the digital era, folders are still used as backup or for systems not yet fully electronic. The price covers the cost of the physical folder and labeling.',

            'FAMILY FOLDER' => 'A Family Folder is a medical record container designed to hold health documents for an entire family unit. It allows healthcare providers to have a holistic view of a family\'s health history, including genetic predispositions and hereditary conditions. The folder often includes sections for each family member, with tabs for easy separation. It is useful for family physicians, pediatricians, and clinics that treat multiple members of the same household. The folder promotes coordinated care and helps in tracking patterns such as familial diseases. It is made of sturdy material and may include a cover page with family demographics. The price reflects the larger size and extra compartments compared to a single folder. It is a practical solution for managing records of couples and their children. It also aids in preventive health by allowing risk assessments based on family history. The folder is reusable and can be updated over time.',

            'EMERGENCY FOLDER' => 'An Emergency Folder is a specialized medical record holder used in emergency departments to quickly organize critical patient information, including trauma notes, triage sheets, laboratory results, and imaging reports. It is designed for rapid access and may feature bright colors or clear labeling to distinguish it from regular folders. The folder often includes forms for vital signs, medication lists, allergies, and emergency contact details. It helps emergency staff streamline the initial assessment and documentation, reducing delays in care. The folder is typically larger and more robust to withstand frequent handling. It may have pockets for small items like identification bands. The price covers the specialized design and additional durability. It is an essential tool in high-acuity settings where every second counts. The folder is reusable but often replaced after use to maintain hygiene. It supports efficient patient flow and accurate handover.',

            'ANTENATAL FOLDER' => 'An Antenatal Folder is a specialized medical record binder for pregnant women, tracking all aspects of their prenatal care. It contains sections for personal details, obstetric history, laboratory results (blood tests, urine tests), ultrasound reports, blood pressure and weight charts, and fetal monitoring records. The folder often includes a birth plan section and education materials. It serves as a comprehensive record that is carried by the patient to each antenatal visit, allowing continuity of care across different providers. The folder is designed to be portable and durable, often with clear plastic sleeves for sonogram images. It is used to monitor maternal and fetal health, identify high-risk pregnancies, and plan for delivery. The price includes the folder, dividers, and often some printed educational inserts. It is an invaluable tool for promoting positive pregnancy outcomes and patient engagement.',

            'GYNAECOLOGIST CONSULT CONSULTATION' => 'This is a consultation fee for a specialist gynaecologist, covering a comprehensive evaluation of the female reproductive system. The consultation includes a detailed medical history, pelvic examination, and discussion of symptoms such as menstrual irregularities, pelvic pain, infertility, or menopausal issues. The gynaecologist may also perform a Pap smear, breast examination, or arrange for further diagnostic tests like ultrasound or colposcopy. The consultation is essential for diagnosing and managing gynaecological conditions, providing contraceptive counselling, and offering preconception advice. The fee covers the specialist\'s time, expertise, and the use of clinic facilities. It does not include the cost of additional investigations or procedures. Patients should bring previous medical records and a list of medications. The consultation is typically scheduled at the clinic and may last 30–60 minutes. It is a critical service for women\'s health maintenance and disease prevention.',

            'GENERAL CONSULTATION' => 'A General Consultation is a medical appointment with a general practitioner (GP) or primary care physician. It covers the assessment of common illnesses, routine health check-ups, chronic disease management, and referral to specialists when needed. The consultation includes a history taking, physical examination, and discussion of treatment options. The GP may order basic laboratory tests or prescribe medications. The fee covers the physician\'s time and the use of consulting rooms. It does not include the cost of investigations or medications. This service is the first point of contact for most patients and is essential for comprehensive primary care. Patients are advised to bring their medical history and current medications. The consultation typically lasts 15–30 minutes. It plays a vital role in early detection and prevention of diseases.',

            'BED SPACE' => 'Bed Space refers to the daily accommodation charge for an inpatient bed in a hospital ward. It includes the bed, linens, basic nursing supervision, and use of common facilities such as toilets and waiting areas. The fee is per day and does not include medical treatments, medications, or specialized nursing care. Bed spaces are available in various categories (general ward, semi-private, private). The price quoted is for a standard bed in a general ward. Patients are admitted for observation, treatment, or recovery after surgery. The hospital provides a safe environment with 24-hour monitoring. The fee covers the operational costs of the ward, including cleaning, utilities, and basic amenities. Patients may be required to provide their own toiletries. The bed space is essential for inpatient care and supports the overall treatment plan.',

            'NURSING CARE' => 'Nursing Care charge covers the professional services provided by registered nurses during a patient\'s hospital stay or in a home care setting. This includes monitoring vital signs, administering medications, wound care, patient education, and providing support with activities of daily living. The fee is typically per day or per shift, depending on the level of care required (e.g., intensive care vs. general care). Nursing care is essential for patient safety, early detection of complications, and ensuring adherence to treatment plans. The charge does not include the cost of medications, consumables, or doctor\'s fees. It reflects the skill and time of the nursing team. In hospitals, nursing care is usually included in the total bed charge, but this line item may be separate in some settings. It is a fundamental component of quality healthcare delivery.',

            'DELIVERY (WITHOUT CONSULTATION)' => 'This is the charge for a vaginal delivery (normal spontaneous delivery) excluding any antenatal consultations or gynaecologist consultation fees. It covers the use of the delivery room, basic delivery equipment, assistance from midwives and obstetric nurses, and the immediate postpartum care of the mother and newborn. The fee includes the management of the first stage of labour, delivery, and placental expulsion. It does not include anaesthesia (epidural), episiotomy repair, or any operative procedures (forceps, vacuum, or caesarean section). Additional costs may apply for extended hospital stay, newborn screening, or specialized neonatal care. The service is provided by experienced birth attendants in a clean and safe environment. Mothers are monitored for complications such as haemorrhage or pre-eclampsia. The price is a standard package for uncomplicated vaginal deliveries. Patients are advised to pre-register and discuss the full cost breakdown with the hospital.',

            'DELIVERY ITEMS' => 'Delivery Items refers to a package of essential supplies used during childbirth, such as sterile drapes, gloves, cord clamps, suction devices, delivery pads, and newborn receiving blankets. It also may include items for the mother (maternity pads, underwear) and for the baby (diapers, wraps). This package ensures that all necessary consumables are available for a clean and safe delivery. The cost is separate from the professional fees and bed charges. The hospital provides these items as part of its delivery service to maintain hygiene and convenience. Patients may also choose to bring their own items, but this fee covers the standard set. The package is designed to meet safety protocols and avoid any last-minute shortages. It is a one-time charge for each delivery. The items are single-use and disposed of properly. This ensures both maternal and neonatal safety during and immediately after delivery.',
        ];

        // First check fertility descriptions, then original descriptions, then fallback
        if (isset($fertilityDescriptions[$name])) {
            return $fertilityDescriptions[$name];
        }

        if (isset($originalDescriptions[$name])) {
            return $originalDescriptions[$name];
        }

        // Fallback for any missing service (should not happen)
        return 'This service is designed to support your health and well-being. For more details, please contact our team or book a consultation.';
    }

